#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Build and test each open pull request's head on this host; post the verdict.

The default branch's protection requires a commit status
(`checks.context`, say `vm/build-and-test`), and this is what produces
it: the checks every agent runs before pushing, run again on the pull
request's head on this host rather than on GitHub Actions. The Merger
merges on that status (`forge-merger`).

    forge-pr-check                       from cron: start a check of the
                                         oldest waiting head, if none runs
    forge-pr-check --post                the same, posting each verdict
    forge-pr-check --worker SHA --pr N   the check itself (started by the
                                         above; also runnable by hand)
    forge-pr-check --worker SHA --pr N --ahead
                                         the check of a Merger prediction
                                         (merge.test_ahead), never posted

**The cron tick returns at once and the check runs detached.**
`forge-cron-run` holds locks while a job runs, so a long test run under
it would starve the rest of the schedule. The worker is started with
its own session and no inherited descriptors, so it doesn't keep them
either.

**`checks.slots` checks at a time** (default 2; `$FORGE_PR_CHECK_SLOTS`
overrides it). A worker holds its slot's lock in the state directory
while it runs, `worker.lock` for slot 1 and `worker-N.lock` for slot N;
its one line names the pull request, head, start and process (`#7
<sha> <start> pid=<pid>`, then ` ahead` for a prediction). Each slot has its
own worktree (`wt`, `wt-N`) and its own build caches (`checks.caches`,
below), so two checks never share a checkout or wait on each other's
cache lock. A tick starts the oldest waiting head that no slot is
running, in the first free slot, and starts nothing when every slot is
held. The kernel drops a lock however its holder dies, so a killed
worker is never reported as running.

**Oldest-waiting first.** A head waits from when a tick first sees it.
Drafts are not checked. A head whose worktree can't be prepared goes
behind every other, and after MAX_ERRORS failures it is given up on,
so one unfetchable ref can't hold the queue. A head already in the
object store is checked out without fetching its ref. One that had to
be fetched and turned out to have moved before its check started is
dropped; the new head is queued in its place. Any other head that
moved is stopped by the next tick (below).

**A run the host broke is no verdict.** A failed step that exited 126
or 127 (its command can't be run: a tool missing from the host's
`PATH`, say) or whose output says `No space left on device` (or
`ENOSPC`) failed for the host, not the head. Such a run records no
verdict and posts nothing more: the head stays waiting, `pending`, its
faults counted (`faults`, apart from a checkout's `errors`), and
`verdicts.log` says `HOST-FAULT` with the step and why. It keeps its
place in the queue, since the fault was not its own, and is tried
again from cron's next tick, not at once; after MAX_ERRORS it is
given up on with `error`. A red verdict says the head is broken, and
its author would otherwise be sent to fix what isn't hers.

**A run of a head that is no longer its pull request's is stopped.**
When a tick finds a slot running a head that no open pull request has
any more (a new push, an update from `main`, or a closed pull request),
it sends SIGTERM to that worker's process group, which its steps are
in, and logs `SUPERSEDED`. The worker logs `STOPPED`, drops the head
from the queue, records no verdict and posts nothing, frees its slot
and chains the next tick, which starts the new head. Otherwise every
push to a pull request being checked kept a slot busy for a whole run
of a commit nobody would merge. A prediction (`ahead`) is never
stopped this way: it is no pull request's head to begin with.

**A head just started isn't started again.** Until its worker has
taken its slot's lock and said who it is, nothing else shows the head
is being checked, and a tick in that window (from cron, an event, or a
worker's chained tick) would start it again in another slot: a
duplicate run and a duplicate post. So the tick that starts a head
stamps `started` on its waiting entry, under the state lock, and
`next_head` skips a head started less than START_GRACE ago. A worker
that dies before taking its lock leaves its head pickable again once
the grace is up.

**With `checks.first_authors`, those agents' pull requests go first**:
a waiting head of a pull request by one of them is started before any
other pull request's, the Merger's included, and so is a cheap one in
the fast lane. The pulls API's author (`user.login`) is kept with the
head. A head that failed to prepare still goes behind.

**With `labels.urgent`, a pull request carrying that label goes first**,
before `first_authors` and before overdue heads (`max_wait_minutes`),
the oldest of several first, in the slots and in the fast lane. The
pulls API's labels are kept with the head; taking the label off puts it
back in its ordinary place. A head that failed to prepare still goes
behind, and the default branch's traced run still goes first when due.

**With `checks.max_wait_minutes`, no head waits on priority for ever**:
a pull request's head that has waited that long goes before every
other pull request's, the oldest first, the first_authors', the
Merger's own head and its predictions included, and the `STARTED` line
ends `, first: it has waited N minutes`. A head that failed to prepare
is never overdue, and the default branch's traced run still goes first
when due or urgent. Without it, a queue kept busy by the Merger's head
and predictions could leave an ordinary head waiting for hours.

**With `checks.merger_first` on, the Merger's pull request goes first**
(default off). The Merger writes `next.json` in its state directory
each tick, naming the pull request it is waiting on (`--merger-dir`,
default ~/.local/state/agent-forge/<project>/merger). When a head of
that pull request is waiting, isn't given up on (MAX_ERRORS), and no
slot is running or has just started it, it is started before every
other, since the next merge waits on its verdict; the `STARTED` line
then ends `, first: the Merger waits on it`. Otherwise heads are
started oldest first, as without it. **It only orders: nothing is ever
held.** A free slot is never kept empty for the Merger's pull request,
because the Merger names one for any decision that isn't a hand-back
or a skip, including a wait that no check here will end (a CI workflow
that hasn't finished, say), and its `next.json` stays fresh all the
while: holding the slots would stop every other check, the ones
reviewers approve on included. A `next.json` older than MERGER_FRESH,
which a Merger that has stopped leaves behind, names nothing.

**With `merge.test_ahead`, it also tests the Merger's predictions**
(default off; it implies `merger_first`). The Merger writes, in
`ahead.json` beside `next.json`, the commit each of the next few pull
requests in its queue will have once the ones in front of it merge
(`forge-merger` says how). A tick starts, in this order:

1. a head of the pull request in `next.json`, as `merger_first` does;
2. the first entry in `ahead.json` that no slot is running or has just
   started, that hasn't failed to check out MAX_ERRORS times, that is
   not the head's own tree (`same_as_head`), and whose tree has no
   verdict, green or red, from this host and step list; it logs
   `STARTED-AHEAD`;
3. every other waiting head, oldest first.

Nothing after an entry whose tree is red here is started: every later
prediction contains that pull request, so it would only be red too.

An entry is checked out from the object store its worktree shares with
the Merger's checkout, so nothing is fetched and nothing is pushed; the
Merger and this tool must therefore use the same checkout (`repo` in
`ahead.json`), and entries from another are ignored. It is run like a
head, recorded with `ahead` set, logged `VERDICT AHEAD`, and posted
nowhere, since it is no pull request's head. The list is short and each
tree is run once, so the predictions delay other pull requests' checks
by at most `depth` + 1 runs each time the queue or `main` moves.

**With `merge.test_ahead`, a head whose tree is already green here is
not run again.** Each verdict then records the `tree` its commit
names, the `host` it ran on and `steps`, a digest of `checks.steps`.
If an earlier verdict on another commit with the same tree, host and
steps is green, and finished at most `test_ahead.max_age_hours`
(default 24) ago, the worker records the head green with `matched`
naming that commit, logs `MATCHED`, and posts `success` as `green on
<host>: tree tested as <sha> (<whose>)`, naming the Merger's
prediction or the other head. A matched verdict is dated from the run
it stands in for, so a match of a match still ages out after that run.
A head whose tree a slot is checking right now as the Merger's
prediction for the same pull request isn't started beside it: it waits
(`HELD ...: its tree is being checked now as the Merger's prediction`)
and is matched once that verdict is in.
This is what turns a prediction into a merge: when GitHub's update
produces the predicted tree, the head is posted green at once and the
Merger merges it on its next tick.

What that guarantees, and what it doesn't:

* **Green is posted only for a tree a real run turned green**, here,
  with these steps. A red verdict is never reused, since a flake is how
  a red tree turns green: the head is run. So a prediction GitHub's
  update doesn't reproduce costs only its own run, and nothing else is
  posted green.
* **A flaky test is accepted once per tree, not once per head.** A
  green verdict on a tree an earlier verdict here called red logs
  `FLAKE`, so that can be seen.
* **The steps must depend on the tree alone.** A matched head was never
  checked out itself; a prediction's history is not the head's (its
  parent is a stand-in for a squash commit). A step that reads commit
  messages, authors or history (`git log`, `git describe`) can pass on
  one commit and fail on another with the same tree; a project with
  such a step should leave `test_ahead` off, or run that check in
  `merge.gate`, which runs on the real head.
* **The step digest can't see a toolchain upgrade** on the host, which
  is why a verdict counts for `max_age_hours` and no longer.

**With `checks.depends`, a pull request runs only the steps that read
what it changes** (docs/designs/0005; default off). Each new head of
the default branch, as the cron checkout last fetched it, is queued
(`QUEUED main <sha>`) and run with every step under a tracer
(`checks.tracer`): `preload`, agent-forge's lib/forge-trace.c built
with the host's C compiler into the state directory and loaded with
`LD_PRELOAD`, or `strace`. It is posted on that commit and logged
`MAIN-GREEN` or `MAIN-RED`. A green run writes `depends/<tree>.json`:
for each step, the paths inside the worktree it opened, executed or
looked for (`reads`) and the directories it read from or listed
(`dirs`), with the commit, the step digest and the time. Records are
kept 14 days, and the newest always. A red run records nothing; so does
one where a step's trace doesn't even show its own program
(`DEPENDS-UNUSABLE`), or where the tracer can't be used (`NO-TRACE`).
A default-branch head waits behind pull requests' heads, except the
newest, which goes first once it has waited `checks.depends_minutes`
(default 30); an older one no slot is running is dropped when a newer
is queued, since the newest serves every pull request based on it or
later.

A pull request's head is compared with its merge base with the
default branch. The record for that base's tree is used, or else the
newest record of an ancestor of it, with what changed between the two
counted as changed too. A step runs when it read a changed path (or
one under a changed directory), when a path was added or deleted in a
directory it read from or listed, when it is marked `always`, or when
the record doesn't know it. Every step runs, and the status says why,
when there is no merge base, no record for these steps or for an
ancestor, or the diff fails. The steps that run get `CHECK_BASE`, the
merge base, and `CHECK_DEPENDS`, the record's path, so a project's own
runner can narrow further; a step run because every step runs gets
neither. The verdict records `selection` (what ran and why, what was
skipped), and the status says it: `green on <host>: 2 of 6 steps
(docs: designs/0042.md; ...)`. A green verdict on the same tree is
reused for another head only if it ran every step that head needs,
from the same merge base or an ancestor of the head's (a run from an
older base counted more as changed, so it ran a superset: the Merger's
second prediction and on); a match keeps the selection it stands for.

**`checks.fast_lane` (`{"max_seconds": 60}`) adds a slot for cheap
heads** (designs/0006; only with `depends`). Each tick works out, in
the cron checkout, the steps each waiting head would run, as the
worker does, fetching the head there if it hasn't it; the answer is
kept with the head in `state.json` (`lane_steps`). A step's cost is
the median of its last COST_RUNS runs here, from the verdicts'
`step_seconds`; with fewer than COST_MIN_RUNS it has none. A head is
cheap when every step it would run has a cost and they add up to at
most `max_seconds`; one whose steps can't be worked out is not. The
lane runs only cheap heads, oldest first, under the same memory limit
and CPU weight, with its own lock, worktree and caches
(`worker-lane.lock`, `wt-lane`, `<cache>-lane`). An ordinary slot
takes a cheap head only when no other head waits. A lane run whose
steps take over SLOW_LANE times `max_seconds` is not stopped, but
logged `SLOW-LANE` with each step's time. `worker.log` names the lane
(`=== ... fast lane ===`); what is posted is the same.

**With `checks.merge_default`, a pull request's head is checked merged
with the default branch's tip** (designs/0007; default off), the tip
as the cron checkout last fetched it. Unless the head already contains
the tip, the worker commits `git merge-tree --write-tree` of the two,
with both as parents and a fixed identity and date (`merge_default`),
checks that out, and runs the steps there: so the steps and the tree
they run on are of one snapshot, and the tree is the one GitHub's
`update-branch` makes. The verdict is still the head's and posted on
it; it records the tip as `base` and the merge as `merged`, and its
`tree` is the merge's, so with `test_ahead` the Merger's update of the
head is matched to it, unrun, while the tip hasn't moved. With
`depends`, the steps are chosen from the tip, which is `CHECK_BASE`.
The description says `with <branch> at <sha>`, and `(itself red
here)` on a red verdict when the tip's own run here was red. A head
that conflicts with the tip runs nothing: it is recorded red
(`failed` is `merge`, `conflicts` the paths) and posted `conflicts
with <branch> at <sha>`, naming the first few paths. Predictions and
the default branch's own heads are checked as they are.

**A head from another repository is refused.** The steps run the pull
request's own code with this user's access to the host, so only
branches of this repository are run.

**The steps are the configuration's**, `checks.steps`: each
`{"name": ..., "run": [argv], "only_if": path}`, `only_if` naming a
file the head must have for the step to run (default: one step, `make
test`). The list comes from the cron checkout, so a pull request can't
change which checks it is judged by; what each target does is the
head's own. Each step runs even if an earlier one failed, and none is
retried. They run in a clean, detached worktree of the head (`wt` in
the state directory), without `GH_TOKEN` or any `FORGE_` setting, so a
test can't behave differently here for having one. `checks.caches`
maps an environment variable to a directory name, such as
`{"CARGO_TARGET_DIR": "cargo-target"}`: each slot gets its own such
directory in the state directory, outside the worktree, so a head isn't
built from nothing each time. A variable the host already sets wins.
A step is stopped after `checks.step_timeout_minutes` (default 30).

**Each step runs under a memory limit**, in its own `systemd-run
--user --scope` with `MemoryMax` (`checks.memory_max`, default `16G`;
`$FORGE_PR_CHECK_MEMORY_MAX` overrides it; `off` for none) and no swap.
Its `OOMPolicy` is `continue`, so a test that runs out is killed alone:
the step fails with that test's output, rather than systemd stopping
the whole step without a word. A worker that can't start a user scope
says so in `worker.log` and runs its steps without the limit.

**With `checks.reuse`, a step isn't run on inputs it has passed on**
(designs/0009). Each step that applies to a pull request's head gets an
input digest from the dependency record its steps were chosen by: what
it read at the tested tree (the merge, with `merge_default`), what the
directories it listed hold, CHECK_BASE's tree, its configuration entry
and its `version` command's output, and the earlier steps' digests when
it read a path the tree doesn't have (a step's output, or nothing). A
chosen step whose digest the newest verdict here with that digest ran
green, at most `max_age_days` ago, is recorded as reused from that run
(`reused`, the run it stands for) and not run; red, a step marked
`always`, a failing `version` command and a step the record doesn't
know always run. The description names the first reuse. Runs of the
default branch never reuse.

**With `checks.fresh` (`{"wait_minutes": 15}`), every green run keeps
the records fresh** (designs/0010; only with `depends`). A pull
request's run, and a prediction's, is traced too, and when green
writes a record for the tree it checked (the merge, with
`merge_default`), marked `"source": "run"`, with `from` saying where
each step's paths came from: a step it ran gives its trace, a step it
skipped the paths of the record it was chosen from, and a step that
doesn't say `"reads_base": false` nothing, since one given CHECK_BASE
may narrow its run. Such a record may lack steps. It never replaces the
default branch's own record for its tree, and adds to another run's.
Records are kept as before, and at most the newest FRESH_KEEP of each
step. Each step is then chosen from its own record (`choose_fresh`):
the first, of the base's tree's records and then the newest
FRESH_CANDIDATES others, in which nothing the step read changed
between that record's tree and the base's, an ancestor or not; the
head's own changes are counted against it. A step no record answers
for is stale, and runs; the selection lists it in `stale`, and the
steps get the records chosen as one file, `depends/.selection-<slot>.json`,
as CHECK_DEPENDS. The default branch's head is queued only when a step
is stale at its tree or no record has it (`QUEUED ... stale: ...`); it
goes first while a waiting head, estimated against the tip, runs a
step only because it is stale (though not before the Merger's head
when that has no stale step), and otherwise as before; while a head
is held for it, or one is urgent, the Merger's predictions don't go
before it either. Such a head
waits (`HELD`, and `held` in `state.json`) while the tip waits for its
run or is running it, for at most `wait_minutes` from when it was
first seen, then runs as now. Each tick estimates every waiting head
again whenever the tip or the records change, so a held head is
chosen again once a fresh record is written, and goes to the fast lane
if it is then cheap. The Merger's head and a cheap head never wait.

**With `checks.cache_limit` (say `"40G"`), each run prunes its slot's
build caches** while it still holds the slot, since nothing else trims
them and a build tool rarely does: test executables directly in a
`deps` directory older than a day that the default branch's last green
run in the slot didn't write go first, then `incremental` directories
untouched for 7 days, then the oldest files until the slot's caches
hold at most the limit. Links are never followed. What went is logged
as `CACHE-PRUNED`; a failure to prune is logged and changes nothing
else. Without it, nothing is pruned.

**Each step gets `CHECK_RESULTS`, an empty directory of its own** to
leave results in, such as a test runner's per-shard times. It is
`results/<head>/<step>` in the state directory, kept as long as the
verdict (KEEP), and the verdict's `results` names it. A step that
leaves more than RESULTS_MAX bytes keeps none, logged as
`RESULTS-DROPPED`; one that leaves nothing has no directory.

**Each step gets `CHECK_CPUS`, its slot's share of the CPUs**: those
this process may run on, divided by the slots (the fast lane counting
as one more), and at least 1. A test runner that starts a process or a
thread per CPU would otherwise start that many in every slot at once,
and with the agents' own builds, oversubscribe the host. A value the
host already sets wins.

**Each step's scope has a high CPU weight** (`checks.cpu_weight`,
default 1000 against systemd's 100; 0 for none), so on a host where
the agents build too, the check, which every merge waits on, gets most
of the CPU when they compete, and theirs when it is idle.

**Without `--post` it posts nothing.** It reads the open pull requests
as the poster's App either way (`checks.poster`, default the Merger),
minting its token with `forge-app-token`.

**`--post` writes the status** as that App, which needs *Commit
statuses: write*: `pending` when the steps start, then `success`, or
`failure` naming the first failed step. A head refused or given up on
gets `error`, saying why, since the Merger would otherwise wait on it
for a status that never comes. The Merger counts only statuses that App
posted. A post GitHub refuses changes neither the verdict nor the exit
status: each tick posts again any verdict on a still-open head that
never reached GitHub. A worker killed mid-run leaves `pending`, and its
head still waiting, so the next check replaces it.

**A posting worker starts the next tick when it lets the lock go**,
through `forge-cron-run`, so a head that arrived while it ran waits for
that rather than for the next cron minute. A tick with nothing to do
starts nothing, which ends the chain. A head that could not be checked
out is still waiting, so it is left for cron's next tick rather than
retried at once.

The state directory (`--state-dir`, default
~/.local/state/agent-forge/<project>/pr-check) holds `state.json` (the
queue, and each verdict for KEEP), `verdicts.log` (one line per
verdict, refusal and give-up), and `worker.log`, each worker's output:
every step's exit status and time, and under a failed step its last
TAIL_LINES lines, each prefixed `    | `. The status names the failed
step and nothing more; why it failed is in `worker.log`.

The checkout is the cron checkout (`--checkout`, default
`$FORGE_CRON_CHECKOUT`), kept at the default branch.

Exit 0 when the tick ran or the check was green, 1 from a red check,
and 2 when the question could not be asked.
"""

import argparse
import datetime
import hashlib
import json
import os
import re
import resource
import shutil
import signal
import socket
import statistics
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import threading
import time

sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))))
from agent_forge import config, github, locks, trace  # noqa: E402

BIN = os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))

# Set from the project's configuration by `configure`; these are the
# defaults it starts from.
REPO_SLUG = ""
MAIN = "main"
# The configuration file, for forge-app-token, whatever the cwd.
CONFIG_PATH = None
# The commit status this posts, and the App it posts and reads as.
DEFAULT_CONTEXT = "vm/build-and-test"
STATUS_CONTEXT = DEFAULT_CONTEXT
POSTER = "Merger"
HOST = ""
# What the worker runs, in order: (name, argv, run only if this path
# exists in the head, or None).
DEFAULT_STEPS = [("test", ["make", "test"], None)]
STEPS = DEFAULT_STEPS
# {environment variable: directory name} for each slot's build caches.
CACHES = {}
# checks.reuse (designs/0009): how old a step's green verdict may be to
# stand for the same inputs, or None: never reused.
REUSE_AGE = None
DEFAULT_REUSE_DAYS = 7
# Each step's `version` command, by name, and its configuration entry
# as canonical JSON, both part of its input digest.
VERSIONS = {}
# Steps whose digest leaves CHECK_BASE out (`"reads_base": false`).
NO_BASE = frozenset()
STEP_ENTRIES = {}
# How long a step's `version` command may take.
VERSION_TIMEOUT_S = 60
# checks.cache_limit: the most a slot's caches may hold, in bytes, after
# a run prunes them (`prune_caches`); None: never pruned.
CACHE_LIMIT = None
# What pruning takes first: executables under a `deps` directory older
# than this that neither this run nor the default branch's last green
# run in the slot wrote, and `incremental` directories untouched for
# longer than this.
STALE_EXECUTABLE = datetime.timedelta(days=1)
STALE_INCREMENTAL = datetime.timedelta(days=7)
SIZE_UNITS = {"": 1, "K": 1 << 10, "M": 1 << 20, "G": 1 << 30, "T": 1 << 40}
DEFAULT_STEP_TIMEOUT_S = 30 * 60
STEP_TIMEOUT_S = DEFAULT_STEP_TIMEOUT_S
# Each step's memory limit, in systemd's syntax; FORGE_PR_CHECK_MEMORY_MAX
# overrides it, and "off" runs the steps without one.
DEFAULT_MEMORY_MAX = "16G"
MEMORY_MAX = DEFAULT_MEMORY_MAX
# Each step's scope's CPUWeight (systemd's default is 100); 0 sets none.
DEFAULT_CPU_WEIGHT = 1000
CPU_WEIGHT = DEFAULT_CPU_WEIGHT
# Lines of a failed step's output kept in worker.log.
TAIL_LINES = 40
# Verdicts older than this are dropped from state.json.
KEEP = datetime.timedelta(days=21)
# Each run's CHECK_RESULTS directories, by head, in the state directory:
# kept as long as the run's verdict, each step's at most RESULTS_MAX
# bytes. One with no verdict (a run stopped, or the host's fault) goes
# once it is RESULTS_ORPHAN_AGE old, which no run takes.
RESULTS_DIR = "results"
RESULTS_MAX = 10 * 1024 * 1024
RESULTS_ORPHAN_AGE = datetime.timedelta(days=1)
# Failures to prepare a head's worktree before it is given up on.
MAX_ERRORS = 3
# How long a head just started is left to its worker before another
# tick may start it: longer than a worker takes to take its lock.
START_GRACE = datetime.timedelta(seconds=60)
# Checks run at once; FORGE_PR_CHECK_SLOTS overrides it.
DEFAULT_SLOTS = 2
SLOTS = DEFAULT_SLOTS
# GitHub refuses a status description longer than this.
DESCRIPTION_MAX = 140
# The most unposted verdicts one tick posts again, oldest first.
POST_BATCH = 10
# Never passed to the steps: they must not act on GitHub, and a test in
# the head must not find this tool's state or settings. Nor is any
# variable starting STEP_DROP_PREFIX.
STEP_DROP_ENV = ("GH_TOKEN", "GITHUB_TOKEN")
STEP_DROP_PREFIX = "FORGE_"
# checks.drop_env: the project's own, added to those.
DROP_NAMES = frozenset()
DROP_PREFIXES = ()
CRON_RUN = os.path.join(BIN, "forge-cron-run")
# What a posting worker starts when it is done: its own cron line.
NEXT_TICK = ("forge-pr-check", "--post")
# The tick's own --merger-dir, when its cron line names one: handed to
# each worker, so the tick a worker chains reads the same Merger.
MERGER_DIR_ARG = None
# Whether the head of the pull request the Merger waits on goes first.
# It only orders: no slot is ever kept for it.
DEFAULT_MERGER_FIRST = False
# checks.first_authors: the bot logins whose pull requests go first.
FIRST_AUTHORS = frozenset()
# labels.urgent: the label that puts a pull request first, or None.
URGENT_LABEL = None
# checks.max_wait_minutes: how long a pull request's head waits behind
# others' priority before it goes first itself, or None.
MAX_WAIT = None
MERGER_FIRST = DEFAULT_MERGER_FIRST
# How old the Merger's next.json and ahead.json may be and still be
# read. An older one means the Merger has stopped, and a stopped Merger
# must not keep a pull request's place at the front.
MERGER_FRESH = datetime.timedelta(minutes=10)
# `merge.test_ahead`: whether the Merger's predictions are tested and a
# head whose tree is green here already is posted green without a run,
# and for how long a verdict counts for another commit with its tree.
TEST_AHEAD = False
# merge.test_ahead.spare_slots_only (designs/0014): predictions only when
# no ordinary head is waiting.
SPARE_ONLY = False
DEFAULT_MATCH_MAX_HOURS = 24
MATCH_MAX_AGE = datetime.timedelta(hours=DEFAULT_MATCH_MAX_HOURS)
# `checks.depends` (docs/designs/0005): each new head of the default
# branch is run in full under a tracer, recording what each step
# reads, and a pull request runs the steps that read what it changes.
DEPENDS = False
# How long the newest default-branch head waits behind pull requests
# before it goes first.
DEFAULT_DEPENDS_MINUTES = 30
DEPENDS_WAIT = datetime.timedelta(minutes=DEFAULT_DEPENDS_MINUTES)
TRACER = trace.DEFAULT_TRACER
# The steps that run whatever changed (`always`).
ALWAYS = frozenset()
# checks.fast_lane (designs/0006): one more slot, for heads whose chosen
# steps have together taken at most FAST_LANE_S here; None is off.
DEFAULT_FAST_LANE_S = 60
FAST_LANE_S = None
# A step's cost is the median of its last COST_RUNS runs here; with
# fewer than COST_MIN_RUNS it has none, and counts as expensive.
COST_RUNS = 20
COST_MIN_RUNS = 5
# The fast lane's slot: its lock, worktree and caches end in -lane.
LANE = 0
# A run in the lane longer than this many times FAST_LANE_S is logged
# SLOW-LANE, and not stopped.
SLOW_LANE = 3
# checks.merge_default (designs/0007): a pull request's head is checked
# merged with the default branch's tip. The merge is a local commit
# with a fixed identity and date, so the same inputs give the same SHA.
MERGE_DEFAULT = False
# checks.fresh (designs/0010): every green run is traced and writes a
# record, each step is chosen from its own freshest record, and a head
# that would run a step only because its record is stale waits up to
# FRESH_WAIT for a fresh one. None is off.
DEFAULT_FRESH_MINUTES = 15
FRESH_WAIT = None
# checks.main "every" (designs/0011): every new default-branch head is
# run, depends or not; otherwise only when a dependency record needs it.
MAIN_EVERY = False
# checks.main_red_issue (designs/0011): {"label", "title"}, or None.
MAIN_RED_ISSUE = None
DEFAULT_RED_TITLE = "The default branch is red at {sha}"
# With fresh, how many of the newest records each step keeps, and how
# many of the newest records a step may be chosen from.
FRESH_KEEP = 20
FRESH_CANDIDATES = 20
MERGE_DATE = "2000-01-01T00:00:00Z"
MERGE_IDENTITY = ("forge-pr-check", "pr-check@agent-forge.invalid")


def configure(project):
    """Set the module's settings from `project`, a loaded configuration:
    its repository and default branch, and the `checks` section
    (`context`, `poster`, `steps`, `caches`, `slots`, `memory_max`,
    `step_timeout_minutes`, `merger_first`, `depends`,
    `depends_minutes`, `tracer`, `fast_lane`, `merge_default`,
    `cache_limit`, `reuse`, `fresh`, `first_authors`, `max_wait_minutes`,
    each step's `always` and `version`),
    each with the
    default above, and `merge.test_ahead` (`max_age_hours`), which
    implies `merger_first`."""
    global REPO_SLUG, MAIN, CONFIG_PATH, STATUS_CONTEXT, POSTER, HOST
    global STEPS, CACHES, SLOTS, MEMORY_MAX, STEP_TIMEOUT_S, MERGER_FIRST
    global CPU_WEIGHT
    global TEST_AHEAD, SPARE_ONLY, MATCH_MAX_AGE, DEPENDS, DEPENDS_WAIT, TRACER, ALWAYS
    global FAST_LANE_S, MERGE_DEFAULT, CACHE_LIMIT
    global REUSE_AGE, VERSIONS, STEP_ENTRIES, NO_BASE, FRESH_WAIT
    global FIRST_AUTHORS, MAX_WAIT, MAIN_EVERY, MAIN_RED_ISSUE, URGENT_LABEL
    global DROP_NAMES, DROP_PREFIXES
    checks = project.section("checks")
    REPO_SLUG, MAIN = project.repo, project.default_branch
    CONFIG_PATH = project.path
    STATUS_CONTEXT = checks.get("context", DEFAULT_CONTEXT)
    POSTER = checks.get("poster",
                        project.section("merge").get("merger", "Merger"))
    HOST = project.host
    STEPS = [(s["name"], list(s["run"]), s.get("only_if"))
             for s in checks["steps"]] if "steps" in checks else DEFAULT_STEPS
    ALWAYS = frozenset(s["name"] for s in checks.get("steps", [])
                       if s.get("always"))
    VERSIONS = {s["name"]: list(s["version"])
                for s in checks.get("steps", []) if s.get("version")}
    # Steps that say they never read CHECK_BASE: their digests leave the
    # base out, so they can be reused across the default branch moving.
    NO_BASE = frozenset(s["name"] for s in checks.get("steps", [])
                        if s.get("reads_base") is False)
    STEP_ENTRIES = {s["name"]: json.dumps(s, sort_keys=True)
                    for s in checks.get("steps", [])}
    # The configuration refuses reuse without depends.
    REUSE_AGE = datetime.timedelta(days=checks["reuse"].get(
        "max_age_days", DEFAULT_REUSE_DAYS)) if "reuse" in checks else None
    DEPENDS = checks.get("depends", False)
    DEPENDS_WAIT = datetime.timedelta(minutes=checks.get(
        "depends_minutes", DEFAULT_DEPENDS_MINUTES))
    TRACER = checks.get("tracer", trace.DEFAULT_TRACER)
    # The configuration refuses fast_lane without depends.
    FAST_LANE_S = checks["fast_lane"].get("max_seconds", DEFAULT_FAST_LANE_S) \
        if "fast_lane" in checks else None
    MERGE_DEFAULT = checks.get("merge_default", False)
    # Their Apps' logins, as the pulls API reports a pull request's
    # author; the configuration refuses a name that isn't an agent.
    FIRST_AUTHORS = frozenset(project.bot_login(name).lower()
                              for name in checks.get("first_authors", []))
    MAX_WAIT = datetime.timedelta(minutes=checks["max_wait_minutes"]) \
        if "max_wait_minutes" in checks else None
    URGENT_LABEL = project.section("labels").get("urgent")
    MAIN_EVERY = checks.get("main", "needed") == "every"
    MAIN_RED_ISSUE = dict(checks["main_red_issue"]) \
        if "main_red_issue" in checks else None
    # The configuration refuses fresh without depends.
    FRESH_WAIT = datetime.timedelta(minutes=checks["fresh"].get(
        "wait_minutes", DEFAULT_FRESH_MINUTES)) if "fresh" in checks else None
    CACHES = dict(checks.get("caches", {}))
    drop = checks.get("drop_env", [])
    DROP_NAMES = frozenset(d for d in drop if not d.endswith("*"))
    DROP_PREFIXES = tuple(d[:-1] for d in drop if d.endswith("*"))
    CACHE_LIMIT = size_bytes(checks["cache_limit"]) \
        if "cache_limit" in checks else None
    SLOTS = checks.get("slots", DEFAULT_SLOTS)
    MEMORY_MAX = checks.get("memory_max", DEFAULT_MEMORY_MAX)
    CPU_WEIGHT = checks.get("cpu_weight", DEFAULT_CPU_WEIGHT)
    STEP_TIMEOUT_S = 60 * checks.get("step_timeout_minutes",
                                     DEFAULT_STEP_TIMEOUT_S // 60)
    ahead = project.section("merge").get("test_ahead")
    TEST_AHEAD = ahead is not None
    SPARE_ONLY = bool((ahead or {}).get("spare_slots_only"))
    MATCH_MAX_AGE = datetime.timedelta(hours=(ahead or {}).get(
        "max_age_hours", DEFAULT_MATCH_MAX_HOURS))
    # The configuration refuses test_ahead with merger_first false.
    MERGER_FIRST = checks.get("merger_first", DEFAULT_MERGER_FIRST) \
        or TEST_AHEAD


def size_bytes(text):
    """A size in systemd's style ("40G", "512M", "100") as bytes."""
    text = text.strip().upper()
    unit = text[-1:] if text[-1:] in SIZE_UNITS else ""
    return int(text[:len(text) - len(unit)]) * SIZE_UNITS[unit]


def sh(args, cwd=None, env=None, timeout=None):
    """Run args; a timeout comes back as exit 124, like timeout(1)."""
    try:
        return subprocess.run(args, cwd=cwd, env=env, capture_output=True,
                              text=True, timeout=timeout)
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired as err:
        out = err.stdout if isinstance(err.stdout, str) else ""
        return subprocess.CompletedProcess(
            args, 124, out, "timed out after %ss" % timeout)


def now_utc():
    return datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc).replace(microsecond=0)


def iso(when):
    return when.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ")


def parse(stamp):
    return datetime.datetime.strptime(stamp, "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ").replace(
        tzinfo=datetime.timezone.utc)


def state_lock(state_dir):
    """Held around every read-modify-write of state.json, which the tick
    and the worker both write."""
    return locks.locked(os.path.join(state_dir, "state.lock"))


def load(state_dir):
    try:
        with open(os.path.join(state_dir, "state.json")) as handle:
            state = json.load(handle)
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        state = {}
    for key in ("waiting", "results", "refused"):
        state.setdefault(key, {})
    return state


def save(state_dir, state):
    """Written whole and renamed into place, so a reader never sees half."""
    fd, tmp = tempfile.mkstemp(dir=state_dir, prefix=".state.")
    with os.fdopen(fd, "w") as handle:
        json.dump(state, handle, indent=1, sort_keys=True)
    os.replace(tmp, os.path.join(state_dir, "state.json"))


def log(state_dir, line, when, out=print):
    """One line to verdicts.log, and to out."""
    text = "%s %s" % (iso(when), line)
    with open(os.path.join(state_dir, "verdicts.log"), "a") as handle:
        handle.write(text + "\n")
    out(text)


try_lock = locks.try_lock


def slot_name(name, slot):
    """`name` for slot 1, which keeps the one-slot names, name-lane for
    the fast lane, else name-N."""
    if slot == LANE:
        return name + "-lane"
    return name if slot == 1 else "%s-%d" % (name, slot)


def slot_label(slot):
    """How the logs name a slot."""
    return "the fast lane" if slot == LANE else "slot %d" % slot


def lock_path(state_dir, slot=1):
    return os.path.join(state_dir, slot_name("worker", slot) + ".lock")


def slots_from_env(environ=None):
    """FORGE_PR_CHECK_SLOTS as an int of at least 1, else SLOTS."""
    value = (os.environ if environ is None else environ).get(
        "FORGE_PR_CHECK_SLOTS", "")
    return int(value) if value.isascii() and value.isdigit() \
        and int(value) >= 1 else SLOTS


def running(state_dir, slot=1):
    """The slot's lock holder line while someone holds it ("" if it
    hasn't said who), or None when it is free."""
    return locks.holder(lock_path(state_dir, slot))


def holder_fields(line):
    """(pr, sha, started, pid, ahead) from a worker's lock line, or None
    for a line that doesn't name a check. pid is None in a line from
    before lock lines named it. `ahead` is true for any run that is no
    pull request's head, a prediction or a default-branch head, which
    nothing supersedes."""
    parts = (line or "").split()
    if len(parts) < 3 or not parts[0].startswith("#") \
            or not parts[0][1:].isdigit():
        return None
    pid = None
    for part in parts[3:]:
        if part.startswith("pid=") and part[4:].isdigit():
            pid = int(part[4:])
    return (int(parts[0][1:]), parts[1], parts[2], pid,
            "ahead" in parts[3:] or "main" in parts[3:])


def stop_worker(pid):
    """SIGTERM to worker pid's process group, which it leads when it was
    started by a tick (its steps are in it too), else to pid alone.
    Whether a signal was sent."""
    try:
        if os.getpgid(pid) == pid:
            os.killpg(pid, signal.SIGTERM)
        else:
            os.kill(pid, signal.SIGTERM)
    except OSError:
        return False
    return True


class Stopped(BaseException):
    """SIGTERM reached a worker: a tick found its head superseded. A
    BaseException, so no `except Exception` on the way swallows it."""


def _stopped(signum, frame):
    raise Stopped()


def mint(run=sh):
    """POSTER's installation token, or None."""
    env = dict(os.environ)
    if CONFIG_PATH:
        env["FORGE_CONFIG"] = CONFIG_PATH
    got = run([os.path.join(BIN, "forge-app-token"), "--token", POSTER],
              env=env)
    token = (got.stdout or "").strip()
    return token if got.returncode == 0 and token else None


def gh(args, token, run=sh):
    return run(["gh", "api"] + args, env=dict(os.environ, GH_TOKEN=token))


def open_heads(token, run=sh, why=None):
    """[(pr, sha, head repository, draft, author, labels)] for every open
    pull request, or None if GitHub could not be read, with the reason
    appended to `why` (a list) when given. A head whose repository is
    gone reports "" for it, and so does an author GitHub didn't give.
    `labels` is the list of the pull request's label names."""
    got = gh(["--paginate", "repos/%s/pulls?state=open&per_page=100"
              % REPO_SLUG, "--jq", '.[] | [.number, .head.sha, '
              '(.head.repo.full_name // ""), .draft, (.user.login // ""), '
              '([(.labels // [])[].name] | tojson)] | @tsv'], token, run)
    if got.returncode != 0:
        if why is not None:
            said = [l.strip() for l in (got.stderr or "").splitlines()
                    if l.strip()]
            why.append("gh exited %d: %s" % (
                got.returncode, said[-1][:200] if said else "no message"))
        return None
    heads = []
    for line in got.stdout.splitlines():
        fields = line.split("\t")
        if len(fields) not in (4, 5, 6) or not fields[0].isdigit() \
                or len(fields[1]) != 40:
            if why is not None:
                why.append("an unreadable line: %r" % line[:100])
            return None
        try:
            labels = json.loads(fields[5]) if len(fields) > 5 else []
        except ValueError:
            labels = []
        if not isinstance(labels, list):
            labels = []
        heads.append((int(fields[0]), fields[1], fields[2],
                      fields[3] == "true", (fields[4:] or [""])[0],
                      [str(name) for name in labels]))
    return heads


def note_heads(state, heads, when, state_dir, out=print, post=None):
    """Queue each open, non-draft head not yet checked, refuse a foreign
    one (posting why, once, with post), and forget a waiting head that
    is no longer any pull request's.

    A new head that replaces a head of the same pull request still
    waiting here keeps that head's place (its `since`): the pull
    request was waiting all along, and a push, by its author or the
    Merger's update, must not send it to the back of the queue."""
    current, new = set(), []
    for pr, sha, repo, draft, *extra in heads:
        author = extra[0] if extra else ""
        labels = extra[1] if len(extra) > 1 else []
        if draft or sha in state["results"]:
            continue
        if repo != REPO_SLUG:
            if sha not in state["refused"]:
                state["refused"][sha] = {"pr": pr, "repo": repo,
                                         "since": iso(when)}
                where = repo or "a deleted repository"
                log(state_dir, "REFUSED #%d %s: head is in %s, not %s"
                    % (pr, sha[:10], where, REPO_SLUG), when, out)
                if post is not None:
                    post(sha, "error", "not run: the head is in %s, and "
                         "only %s's own branches are run" % (where,
                                                             REPO_SLUG))
            continue
        current.add(sha)
        if sha not in state["waiting"]:
            new.append(sha)
        state["waiting"].setdefault(sha, {"pr": pr, "since": iso(when)})
        state["waiting"][sha]["pr"] = pr
        if author:
            state["waiting"][sha]["author"] = author
        if URGENT_LABEL and URGENT_LABEL in labels:
            state["waiting"][sha]["urgent"] = True
        else:
            state["waiting"][sha].pop("urgent", None)
    replaced = {}
    for sha in list(state["waiting"]):
        entry = state["waiting"][sha]
        # A default-branch head (checks.depends) is no pull request's.
        if sha not in current and not entry.get("main"):
            pr = entry.get("pr")
            if pr is not None and (pr not in replaced
                                   or entry["since"] < replaced[pr]):
                replaced[pr] = entry["since"]
            del state["waiting"][sha]
    for sha in new:
        entry = state["waiting"][sha]
        if entry["pr"] in replaced and replaced[entry["pr"]] < entry["since"]:
            entry["since"] = replaced[entry["pr"]]


def who(pr, main=False):
    """How a run is named in the logs: its pull request, or `main`."""
    return MAIN if main else "#%d" % pr


def main_head(checkout, run=sh):
    """(sha, tree) of the default branch's head as the cron checkout
    last fetched it, or None if git can't say."""
    ref = "refs/remotes/origin/" + MAIN
    got = run(["git", "-C", checkout, "rev-parse", ref, ref + "^{tree}"])
    words = (got.stdout or "").split()
    if got.returncode != 0 or len(words) != 2 \
            or not all(len(w) == 40 for w in words):
        return None
    return words[0], words[1]


def queue_main(state, head, when, state_dir, busy=(), needs=False):
    """Queue the default branch's head (sha, tree) for a traced full run,
    unless it has a verdict, is queued, or its tree has a record. An
    older default-branch head no slot is running is dropped: the newest
    serves every pull request based on it or later. It waits from when
    the oldest it replaces did, or a default branch that moves more
    often than DEPENDS_WAIT would never go first. Whether it was
    queued.

    With checks.fresh, `needs` is what `main_needs` said instead of
    the record's file: a head that needs no step traced isn't queued,
    and nor is an older one kept waiting, since every record holds."""
    sha, tree = head
    if MAIN_EVERY:
        # designs/0011: every head, whatever any record says. A head whose
        # tree a green run here already passed is matched by the worker.
        if sha in state["results"] or sha in state["waiting"]:
            return False
    elif needs is not False:
        if needs == []:
            for other, entry in list(state["waiting"].items()):
                if entry.get("main") and other not in busy:
                    del state["waiting"][other]
            return False
        if sha in state["results"] or sha in state["waiting"]:
            return False
    elif sha in state["results"] or sha in state["waiting"] or \
            os.path.exists(os.path.join(trace.records_dir(state_dir),
                                        tree + ".json")):
        return False
    since = iso(when)
    for other, entry in list(state["waiting"].items()):
        if entry.get("main") and other not in busy:
            since = min(since, entry.get("since", since))
            del state["waiting"][other]
    state["waiting"][sha] = {"pr": 0, "since": since, "main": True}
    return True


def main_due(entry, when):
    """Whether a waiting default-branch head has waited DEPENDS_WAIT. An
    unreadable stamp counts as due, as in next_head."""
    try:
        return when - parse(entry["since"]) >= DEPENDS_WAIT
    except (KeyError, TypeError, ValueError):
        return True


def just_started(entry, when):
    """Whether a waiting entry was started less than START_GRACE before
    `when`. An unreadable stamp counts as not started."""
    try:
        return when is not None and when - parse(entry["started"]) < START_GRACE
    except (KeyError, TypeError, ValueError):
        return False


def live_heads(state, busy=(), when=None):
    """The waiting heads that could be picked: not given up on, not
    running (`busy`), and not started less than START_GRACE before
    `when`."""
    return [s for s, w in state["waiting"].items()
            if w.get("errors", 0) < MAX_ERRORS
            and w.get("faults", 0) < MAX_ERRORS and s not in busy
            and not just_started(w, when)]


def first(entry):
    """Whether a waiting head's pull request is by one of
    `checks.first_authors`."""
    return entry.get("author", "").lower() in FIRST_AUTHORS


def overdue(entry, when):
    """Whether a waiting pull request's head has waited MAX_WAIT
    (checks.max_wait_minutes) or longer by `when`. One that failed to
    prepare never is: it goes behind for a reason of its own."""
    if MAX_WAIT is None or when is None or entry.get("main") \
            or entry.get("errors"):
        return False
    try:
        return when - parse(entry["since"]) >= MAX_WAIT
    except (KeyError, TypeError, ValueError):
        return False


def order(waiting, sha):
    """A head's place in the queue: one that failed to prepare goes
    behind every one that has not, then an urgent one (`labels.urgent`)
    before one that isn't, then one by `checks.first_authors` before one
    that isn't, then the oldest first."""
    return (waiting[sha].get("errors", 0), not waiting[sha].get("urgent"),
            not first(waiting[sha]), waiting[sha]["since"], sha)


def lane_head(state, cheap, busy=(), when=None):
    """(sha, pr) of the cheap head (checks.fast_lane) that has waited
    longest, in next_head's order, or None."""
    waiting = state["waiting"]
    live = [s for s in live_heads(state, busy, when) if s in cheap]
    if not live:
        return None
    sha = min(live, key=lambda s: order(waiting, s))
    return sha, waiting[sha]["pr"]


def next_head(state, busy=(), first_pr=None, when=None, cheap=(),
              urgent=False):
    """(sha, pr) of the head that has waited longest, or None. A head
    that failed to prepare goes behind every head that has not; one
    whose run the host broke keeps its place, since the fault was not
    its own. One that failed either way MAX_ERRORS times is not picked
    at all, and nor is one a slot is running (`busy`) or one a tick
    started less than START_GRACE before `when`.

    `first_pr` is the pull request the Merger is waiting on, or None.
    A head of it that could be picked goes before every other. It only
    orders: when none of its heads can be picked, the rest are picked
    as if it were None (see the module docstring).

    A default-branch head (checks.depends, pr 0) goes after every pull
    request's, except the newest, which goes first of all once it has
    waited DEPENDS_WAIT, or at once when `urgent` (checks.fresh: a
    waiting head runs a step only because its record is stale). Urgent
    is not reason enough to go before the Merger's pull request when
    its head has no stale step: the default branch's run would not
    shorten it, only delay the merge.

    With the fast lane, a head in `cheap` is picked only when no other
    head could be: the lane is for it (`lane_head`)."""
    waiting = state["waiting"]
    live = live_heads(state, busy, when)
    if not live:
        return None
    dear = [s for s in live if s not in cheap]
    if dear:
        live = dear
    mains = [s for s in live if waiting[s].get("main")]
    heads = [s for s in live if not waiting[s].get("main")]
    mine = [s for s in heads if waiting[s]["pr"] == first_pr]
    if urgent and any(not waiting[s].get("stale_steps") for s in mine):
        urgent = False
    if mains:
        newest = max(mains, key=lambda s: (waiting[s]["since"], s))
        try:
            due = when is not None and \
                when - parse(waiting[newest]["since"]) >= DEPENDS_WAIT
        except (TypeError, ValueError):
            due = True
        if due or urgent or not heads:
            return newest, 0
    # labels.urgent: a person has put this pull request first.
    hot = [s for s in heads
           if waiting[s].get("urgent") and not waiting[s].get("errors")]
    if hot:
        sha = min(hot, key=lambda s: order(waiting, s))
        return sha, waiting[sha]["pr"]
    # checks.max_wait_minutes: a head that has waited that long goes
    # before every other pull request's, the oldest first, so priority
    # can't starve it.
    late = [s for s in heads if overdue(waiting[s], when)]
    if late:
        sha = min(late, key=lambda s: (waiting[s]["since"], s))
        return sha, waiting[sha]["pr"]
    # checks.first_authors go even before the Merger's pull request,
    # unless they failed to prepare.
    top = [s for s in heads
           if first(waiting[s]) and not waiting[s].get("errors")]
    sha = min(top or mine or heads, key=lambda s: order(waiting, s))
    return sha, waiting[sha]["pr"]


def merger_first(merger_dir, when):
    """The pull request the Merger last said it waits on, from its
    next.json, or None when there is none, it can't be read, or it is
    older than MERGER_FRESH."""
    if not merger_dir:
        return None
    try:
        with open(os.path.join(merger_dir, "next.json")) as handle:
            said = json.load(handle)
        if when - parse(said["at"]) > MERGER_FRESH:
            return None
        pr = said["pr"]
        # bool is an int to Python, never a pull request number.
        return pr if isinstance(pr, int) and not isinstance(pr, bool) \
            else None
    except (OSError, ValueError, KeyError, TypeError):
        return None


def merger_ahead(merger_dir, when, repo=None):
    """The Merger's predicted commits from its ahead.json, or [] when
    there are none, it can't be read, it is older than MERGER_FRESH, or
    it was written in a checkout other than `repo`, whose object store
    this tool's worktrees would not share. Malformed entries are
    dropped."""
    if not merger_dir:
        return []
    try:
        with open(os.path.join(merger_dir, "ahead.json")) as handle:
            said = json.load(handle)
        if when - parse(said["at"]) > MERGER_FRESH:
            return []
        if repo is not None and os.path.realpath(said["repo"]) \
                != os.path.realpath(repo):
            return []
        return [e for e in said["entries"]
                if isinstance(e.get("pr"), int)
                and not isinstance(e.get("pr"), bool)
                and isinstance(e.get("sha"), str) and len(e["sha"]) == 40
                and isinstance(e.get("tree"), str) and e["tree"]]
    except (OSError, ValueError, KeyError, TypeError, AttributeError):
        return []


def steps_digest(steps=None, always=None):
    """A short digest of the step list, so a verdict or a dependency
    record stands for another commit's only while the steps that made it
    are still the ones run. The steps marked `always` count too, when
    there are any, so a list without them digests as it always has."""
    listed = [[name, list(args), only_if]
              for name, args, only_if in (STEPS if steps is None else steps)]
    always = ALWAYS if always is None else always
    if always:
        listed.append(["always", sorted(always)])
    return hashlib.sha256(json.dumps(listed).encode()).hexdigest()[:16]


def verdict_age(record, when):
    """How long before `when` a verdict finished, or None if its stamp
    can't be read."""
    try:
        return when - parse(record["finished"])
    except (KeyError, TypeError, ValueError):
        return None


def tested(state, tree, when, ok=None, accept=None):
    """The sha of the newest verdict on `tree` from this host and step
    list, green only when `ok`, and only one `accept(record)` takes when
    given, or None. A verdict that finished more than MATCH_MAX_AGE
    before `when`, or after it, doesn't count: the step digest can't see
    a toolchain upgrade."""
    host, steps = socket.gethostname(), steps_digest()
    best = None
    for sha, r in state["results"].items():
        age = verdict_age(r, when)
        if (tree and r.get("tree") == tree and r.get("host") == host
                and r.get("steps") == steps
                and (ok is None or r.get("ok") is ok)
                and (accept is None or accept(r))
                and age is not None
                and datetime.timedelta(0) <= age <= MATCH_MAX_AGE
                and (best is None or r["finished"] > best[1]["finished"])):
            best = (sha, r)
    return best[0] if best else None


def next_ahead(state, ahead, busy=(), when=None, busy_trees=()):
    """(sha, pr) of the first of the Merger's predictions, `ahead`, that
    is worth a run, or None: not running in a slot (`busy`) or started
    less than START_GRACE before `when`, not given up on, not the head's
    own tree, not a tree a slot is checking now (`busy_trees`: two
    predictions can differ only in their parents), and with no verdict
    of its own or on its tree. Nothing
    after an entry whose tree is red here (and not green too, as after a
    flake): each later prediction contains it, so it would be red too,
    and the Merger drops that pull request from the list on its next
    tick anyway."""
    started = state.get("ahead_started", {})
    when = when or now_utc()
    for entry in ahead:
        sha = entry["sha"]
        if tested(state, entry["tree"], when, ok=False) is not None \
                and tested(state, entry["tree"], when, ok=True) is None:
            return None
        if (entry.get("same_as_head") or sha in busy
                or entry["tree"] in busy_trees
                or sha in state["results"]
                or just_started({"started": started.get(sha)}, when)
                or state.get("ahead_errors", {}).get(sha, 0) >= MAX_ERRORS
                or tested(state, entry["tree"], when) is not None):
            continue
        return sha, entry["pr"]
    return None


def forget_ahead(state, ahead):
    """Drop the start stamps and error counts of predictions the Merger
    no longer lists, so they don't pile up."""
    current = {e["sha"] for e in ahead}
    for key in ("ahead_started", "ahead_errors"):
        if key in state:
            state[key] = {s: v for s, v in state[key].items()
                          if s in current}
            if not state[key]:
                del state[key]


def prune(state, when):
    for key, stamp in (("results", "finished"), ("refused", "since")):
        for sha in list(state[key]):
            if when - parse(state[key][sha][stamp]) > KEEP:
                del state[key][sha]


# How long a minted token is used for. GitHub's last an hour; a check
# that runs longer than this mints a fresh one for its verdict.
TOKEN_REUSE_S = 30 * 60


def make_poster(run=sh, out=print, project=None, call=None,
                timer=time.monotonic):
    """A post(sha, state, description) that writes STATUS_CONTEXT on sha
    as POSTER's App and returns whether GitHub took it. Safe to call
    from another thread.

    With `project`, the token is minted and the status written in this
    process (agent_forge.github, through `call`); otherwise through
    forge-app-token and `gh`, each a process of its own, which cost a
    run about a second a post (agent-forge #13). Either way a token is
    reused for TOKEN_REUSE_S, so the pending post and the verdict's
    usually share one."""
    held = {}
    guard = threading.Lock()

    def token():
        with guard:
            if held and timer() - held["at"] < TOKEN_REUSE_S:
                return held["token"]
            if project is not None:
                try:
                    got = github.token(project, POSTER,
                                       call or github.api_call)
                except (github.MintError, OSError, KeyError):
                    got = None
            else:
                got = mint(run)
            if got:
                held.update(token=got, at=timer())
            return got

    def post(sha, state, description):
        tok = token()
        if tok is None:
            out("POST-FAILED %s %s: could not mint %s's token"
                % (sha[:10], state, POSTER))
            return False
        description = description[:DESCRIPTION_MAX]
        if project is not None:
            status, body = github.rest(
                tok, "POST", "/repos/%s/statuses/%s" % (REPO_SLUG, sha),
                {"state": state, "context": STATUS_CONTEXT,
                 "description": description}, call or github.api_call)
            if status != 201:
                out("POST-FAILED %s %s: HTTP %s %s"
                    % (sha[:10], state, status, body.get("message", "")))
                return False
            return True
        got = gh(["-X", "POST", "repos/%s/statuses/%s" % (REPO_SLUG, sha),
                  "-f", "state=" + state, "-f", "context=" + STATUS_CONTEXT,
                  "-f", "description=" + description], tok, run)
        if got.returncode != 0:
            out("POST-FAILED %s %s: %s" % (sha[:10], state,
                                           got.stderr.strip()))
            return False
        return True
    return post


def make_writer(project, out=print, call=None):
    """A write(method, path, body) -> (status, body) to GitHub's REST API
    as POSTER's App (checks.poster), minting its token when first used.
    A token that can't be minted is status 0."""
    held = {}

    def write(method, path, body=None):
        if "token" not in held:
            try:
                held["token"] = github.token(project, POSTER,
                                             call or github.api_call)
            except (github.MintError, OSError, KeyError) as err:
                out("REPORT-FAILED: could not mint %s's token: %s"
                    % (POSTER, err))
                return 0, {}
        return github.rest(held["token"], method, path, body,
                           call or github.api_call)
    return write


def signature():
    """How an issue or comment forge-pr-check writes ends: a line saying
    the machinery wrote it, then `— <poster>`, the App it writes as, the
    last line, which is where a project's own tools read the owner."""
    return ("Written by forge-pr-check, agent-forge's build check.\n\n— %s"
            % POSTER)


def red_text(sha, failed, commits, green):
    """The body of the issue a red default-branch head opens."""
    lines = ["`%s` is red at %s%s: %s." % (
        MAIN, sha[:10], on_host(), ", ".join(n for n, _ in failed)
        or "a step failed")]
    if green:
        lines.append("")
        lines.append("Commits since the last green head, %s:" % green[:10])
        lines.append("")
        lines += ["* %s" % c for c in commits] or ["* (none listed)"]
    for name, text in failed:
        lines += ["", "**%s**, the end of its output:" % name, "",
                  "```", text.rstrip(), "```"]
    lines += ["", "Every pull request's check is against this tip until "
              "it is green again; this issue is closed then.", "",
              signature()]
    return "\n".join(lines)


def report_main(state_dir, repo, sha, ok, failed, same, write, out=print,
                run=sh):
    """checks.main_red_issue (designs/0011): keep one issue open while
    the default branch is red. The first red head opens it (labelled,
    with the failed steps and the commits since the last green head),
    each further red head comments once, and the first green head closes
    it, naming the run whose tree it matched (`same`) if it wasn't run.
    A write GitHub refuses is logged REPORT-FAILED and tried again on the
    next verdict, never dropped."""
    with state_lock(state_dir):
        state = load(state_dir)
        red = dict(state.get("main_red") or {})
        green = state.get("main_green")
    when = now_utc()
    if ok:
        if red.get("issue"):
            number = red["issue"]
            how = ("its tree tested green as %s" % same[:10]) if same \
                else "its check passed"
            got, _ = write("POST", "/repos/%s/issues/%d/comments"
                           % (REPO_SLUG, number), {"body": "Green again at "
                           "%s: %s.\n\n%s" % (sha[:10], how, signature())})
            shut, _ = write("PATCH", "/repos/%s/issues/%d"
                            % (REPO_SLUG, number), {"state": "closed"})
            if shut != 200:
                log(state_dir, "REPORT-FAILED %s %s: closing #%d: HTTP %s"
                    % (MAIN, sha[:10], number, shut), when, out)
                with state_lock(state_dir):
                    state = load(state_dir)
                    state["main_green"] = sha
                    save(state_dir, state)
                return
            log(state_dir, "CLOSED #%d: %s green at %s"
                % (number, MAIN, sha[:10]), when, out)
            red = {}
        with state_lock(state_dir):
            state = load(state_dir)
            state["main_green"] = sha
            state.pop("main_red", None) if not red else None
            save(state_dir, state)
        return
    if red.get("issue"):
        if sha in red.get("told", []):
            return
        number = red["issue"]
        got, _ = write("POST", "/repos/%s/issues/%d/comments"
                       % (REPO_SLUG, number), {"body": "Still red at %s: %s."
                       "\n\n%s" % (sha[:10], ", ".join(
                           n for n, _ in failed) or "a step failed",
                           signature())})
        if got != 201:
            log(state_dir, "REPORT-FAILED %s %s: commenting on #%d: HTTP %s"
                % (MAIN, sha[:10], number, got), when, out)
            return
        red["told"] = red.get("told", []) + [sha]
        log(state_dir, "COMMENTED #%d: %s still red at %s"
            % (number, MAIN, sha[:10]), when, out)
    else:
        commits = []
        if green:
            got = run(["git", "-C", repo, "log", "--format=%h %s",
                       "%s..%s" % (green, sha)])
            if got.returncode == 0:
                commits = got.stdout.splitlines()[:50]
        title = MAIN_RED_ISSUE.get("title", DEFAULT_RED_TITLE).replace(
            "{sha}", sha[:10])
        body = {"title": title, "body": red_text(sha, failed, commits, green)}
        labels = list(MAIN_RED_ISSUE.get("labels", [])) + (
            [MAIN_RED_ISSUE["label"]] if MAIN_RED_ISSUE.get("label") else [])
        if labels:
            body["labels"] = labels
        got, made = write("POST", "/repos/%s/issues" % REPO_SLUG, body)
        if got != 201 or "number" not in made:
            log(state_dir, "REPORT-FAILED %s %s: opening an issue: HTTP %s %s"
                % (MAIN, sha[:10], got, made.get("message", "")), when, out)
            return
        red = {"issue": made["number"], "since": sha, "told": [sha]}
        log(state_dir, "FILED #%d: %s red at %s"
            % (red["issue"], MAIN, sha[:10]), when, out)
    with state_lock(state_dir):
        state = load(state_dir)
        state["main_red"] = red
        save(state_dir, state)


def on_host():
    return " on " + HOST if HOST else ""


def final_status(r):
    """(state, description) for a verdict, naming the first failed step:
    where its author starts reading, or for a matched one where its
    tree was tested. With checks.depends, it says how many steps ran
    and what made each run, or why every step ran; with checks.reuse,
    the first step reused and where it ran. With
    checks.merge_default, it names the tip the head was merged with, or
    the paths that conflict with it."""
    selection = r.get("selection") or {}
    ran, skipped = selection.get("ran", {}), selection.get("skipped", [])
    if r.get("conflicts") is not None:
        paths = r["conflicts"]
        return "failure", "red%s: conflicts with %s at %s%s" % (
            on_host(), MAIN, r["base"][:10],
            ": " + ", ".join(paths[:CONFLICTS_SHOWN])
            + (" and %d more" % (len(paths) - CONFLICTS_SHOWN)
               if len(paths) > CONFLICTS_SHOWN else "") if paths else "")
    # checks.merge_default: what the head was merged with, and whether
    # that tip was red here itself, early, since GitHub cuts the end.
    where = on_host()
    if r.get("base") and not r.get("matched"):
        where += " with %s at %s" % (MAIN, r["base"][:10])
        if not r["ok"] and r.get("base_red"):
            where += " (itself red here)"
    return _final_status(r, selection, ran, skipped, where)


# How many conflicting paths a description names.
CONFLICTS_SHOWN = 3


def _final_status(r, selection, ran, skipped, where):
    if r["ok"] and r.get("matched"):
        return "success", "green%s: tree tested as %s (%s)" % (
            where, r["matched"][:10], r.get("matched_as", "another head"))
    if r["ok"] and r.get("reused"):
        # designs/0009: the first reused step, where it was run, early.
        reused = r["reused"]
        first = next(iter(reused))
        return "success", "green%s: %s, same inputs%s; %d of %d steps ran" % (
            where, reuse_text(first, reused[first]),
            " (and %d more)" % (len(reused) - 1) if len(reused) > 1 else "",
            len(ran) - len(reused), len(ran) + len(skipped))
    if r["ok"] and skipped:
        why = "; ".join("%s: %s" % (n, w) for n, w in ran.items()) \
            or "nothing reads what changed"
        return "success", "green%s: %d of %d steps (%s)" % (
            where, len(ran), len(ran) + len(skipped), why)
    if r["ok"] and selection.get("every"):
        return "success", "green%s in %ds (every step: %s)" % (
            where, r["seconds"], selection["every"])
    if r["ok"]:
        return "success", "green%s in %ds" % (where, r["seconds"])
    failed = r["failed"]
    rest = " (then %s)" % ", ".join(failed[1:]) if failed[1:] else ""
    of = " (%d of %d steps)" % (len(ran), len(ran) + len(skipped)) \
        if skipped else ""
    return "failure", "red%s: %s failed%s%s" % (where, failed[0], rest,
                                               of)


# What a failed step's output says when the host, not the head, broke.
HOST_FAULT_TEXT = ("No space left on device", "ENOSPC")


def host_fault(results):
    """(step, why) for the first failed step the host broke, or None:
    one that exited 126 or 127, so its command could not be run, or
    whose output says the disk is full."""
    for r in results:
        if r["rc"] == 0:
            continue
        if r["rc"] in (126, 127):
            return r["name"], "exit %d: its command could not be run" % r["rc"]
        if any(text in r["tail"] for text in HOST_FAULT_TEXT):
            return r["name"], "no space left on device"
    return None


def count_error(state_dir, sha, ahead, key="errors"):
    """One more error for `sha`, waiting or (`ahead`) predicted; the
    count so far, 0 for a head no longer waiting. A waiting head counts
    failures to prepare it as `errors` and host faults as `faults`:
    only the first send it to the back of the queue (`next_head`)."""
    with state_lock(state_dir):
        state = load(state_dir)
        entry = state["waiting"].get(sha)
        errors = 0
        if ahead:
            counts = state.setdefault("ahead_errors", {})
            errors = counts[sha] = counts.get(sha, 0) + 1
        elif entry is not None:
            errors = entry[key] = entry.get(key, 0) + 1
        save(state_dir, state)
    return errors


def verdict_word(r):
    return "green" if r["ok"] else "red(%s)" % ",".join(r["failed"])


def repost(state, heads, post, out=print):
    """Post again, oldest first, up to POST_BATCH verdicts on open heads
    whose status never reached GitHub."""
    open_shas = {head[1] for head in heads}
    todo = sorted((r["finished"], sha) for sha, r in state["results"].items()
                  if sha in open_shas and r.get("posted") is not True)
    for _, sha in todo[:POST_BATCH]:
        r = state["results"][sha]
        r["posted"] = post(sha, *final_status(r))
        if r["posted"]:
            out("POSTED #%d %s: %s" % (r["pr"], sha[:10], verdict_word(r)))


def spawn_worker(state_dir, sha, pr, posting=False, slot=1, checkout=None,
                 ahead=False, main=False):
    """Start the check detached; see the module docstring on the cron
    lock. Its output goes to worker.log. A prediction (`ahead`) posts
    nothing, but a posting one still chains the next tick. `main` is a
    default-branch head's traced full run (checks.depends)."""
    wlog = open(os.path.join(state_dir, "worker.log"), "a")
    wlog.write("=== %s worker %s %s %s%s ===\n"
               % (iso(now_utc()), who(pr, main), sha,
                  "fast lane" if slot == LANE else "slot %d" % slot,
                  " ahead" if ahead else ""))
    wlog.flush()
    # The installation lock forge-cron-run handed this tick goes on to
    # the worker, which outlives it: the release can't move under a run.
    held = locks.inherited_install_lock()
    subprocess.Popen(
        [sys.executable, os.path.abspath(__file__), "--worker", sha,
         "--pr", str(pr), "--state-dir", state_dir, "--slot", str(slot)]
        + (["--checkout", checkout] if checkout else [])
        + (["--post"] if posting else [])
        + (["--ahead"] if ahead else [])
        + (["--main"] if main else [])
        + (["--merger-dir", MERGER_DIR_ARG] if MERGER_DIR_ARG else []),
        cwd=state_dir, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, stdout=wlog,
        stderr=subprocess.STDOUT, start_new_session=True, close_fds=True,
        pass_fds=() if held is None else (held,))
    wlog.close()


def tick(state_dir, token, run=sh, spawn=spawn_worker, out=print, when=None,
         post=None, slots=None, checkout=None, merger_dir=None,
         stop=stop_worker):
    """Queue the open heads and start the next one in a free slot. With
    `merger_dir` (only with MERGER_FIRST), the Merger's pull request goes
    first; with TEST_AHEAD too, its predictions come next (see the
    module docstring). With FAST_LANE_S, `depends` and a checkout, the
    fast lane is one more slot, which starts the oldest cheap head."""
    when = when or now_utc()
    why = []
    heads = open_heads(token, run, why)
    if heads is None:
        out("NO-PULLS: could not list %s's open pull requests: %s"
            % (REPO_SLUG, why[0] if why else "no reason given"))
        return 2
    slots = slots or slots_from_env()
    lane = FAST_LANE_S is not None and DEPENDS and bool(checkout)
    holders = {slot: running(state_dir, slot)
               for slot in list(range(1, slots + 1)) + ([LANE] if lane else [])}
    fields = {slot: holder_fields(h) for slot, h in holders.items()}
    busy = {f[1] for f in fields.values() if f}
    head_of_main = main_head(checkout, run) \
        if (DEPENDS or MAIN_EVERY) and checkout else None
    fresh = FRESH_WAIT is not None and DEPENDS and head_of_main is not None \
        and not MAIN_EVERY
    # With checks.fresh, what the default branch's head needs traced,
    # worked out outside the lock: it reads git.
    needs = main_needs(checkout, head_of_main, state_dir, run) \
        if fresh else False
    with state_lock(state_dir):
        state = load(state_dir)
        note_heads(state, heads, when, state_dir, out, post)
        if head_of_main and queue_main(state, head_of_main, when, state_dir,
                                       busy, needs):
            out("QUEUED %s %s: a %srun of every step%s"
                % (MAIN, head_of_main[0][:10], "traced " if DEPENDS else "",
                   "" if not needs else "; stale: " + ", ".join(needs)))
        if post is not None:
            repost(state, heads, post, out)
        prune(state, when)
        save(state_dir, state)
        prune_results(state_dir, state, when)
        queued = set(state["waiting"])
    current = {head[1] for head in heads}
    for slot, holder in holders.items():
        if holder is None:
            continue
        got = fields[slot]
        if got and not got[4] and got[1] not in current and got[3]:
            pr, sha, since, pid, _ = got
            sent = stop(pid)
            log(state_dir, "SUPERSEDED #%d %s: no longer the head of any "
                "open pull request; %s the run in %s (pid %d), since %s"
                % (pr, sha[:10], "stopping" if sent else "could not stop",
                   slot_label(slot), pid, since), when, out)
        elif got:
            out("RUNNING #%d %s since %s%s; %d waiting"
                % (got[0], got[1][:10], got[2],
                   " in the fast lane" if slot == LANE else "",
                   len(queued - busy)))
        else:
            out("BUSY: %s is held; %d waiting"
                % ("the worker lock" if slot == 1
                   else os.path.basename(lock_path(state_dir, slot)),
                   len(queued - busy)))
    free = [slot for slot, holder in holders.items()
            if holder is None and slot != LANE]
    lane_free = lane and holders[LANE] is None
    if not free and not lane_free:
        return 0
    if lane or fresh:
        estimate(state_dir, checkout, busy, run,
                 head_of_main[0] if fresh else None)
    first_pr = merger_first(merger_dir, when)
    ahead = (merger_ahead(merger_dir, when, checkout)
             if TEST_AHEAD and merger_dir else [])
    predicted = pick = fast = None
    held = {}
    with state_lock(state_dir):
        state = load(state_dir)
        cheap = cheap_heads(state) if lane else {}
        # A head whose own prediction, the same tree, is running waits
        # for its verdict rather than checking the tree a second time.
        twins = twin_heads(state, fields.values(), checkout, run) \
            if TEST_AHEAD else {}
        if fresh:
            held = held_heads(state, head_of_main[0], busy, when, first_pr,
                              cheap)
            # Why each held head waits, kept with it; dropped once it
            # doesn't.
            for sha, entry in state["waiting"].items():
                if sha in held:
                    entry["held"] = held[sha]
                else:
                    entry.pop("held", None)
            save(state_dir, state)
        # The default branch goes first while a head runs a step only
        # because its record is stale.
        urgent = fresh and any(w.get("stale_steps")
                               and w.get("lane_base") == head_of_main[0]
                               for w in state["waiting"].values()
                               if not w.get("main"))
        if lane_free:
            fast = lane_head(state, cheap, set(busy) | set(twins), when)
            if fast is not None:
                state["waiting"][fast[0]]["started"] = iso(when)
                save(state_dir, state)
        # What the lane just took is as good as running.
        taken = set(busy) | set(twins) | ({fast[0]} if fast else set())
        if free:
            pick = next_head(state, taken | set(held), first_pr, when,
                             cheap, urgent) if fresh else \
                next_head(state, taken, first_pr, when, cheap)
            if TEST_AHEAD:
                forget_ahead(state, ahead)
                # The Merger's predictions go before an ordinary pick,
                # but not before a head that has waited max_wait, nor
                # before the default branch's run once it has waited
                # depends_minutes or while heads wait on its record
                # (urgent, or held). With spare_slots_only
                # (designs/0014), never before an ordinary pick.
                if pick is None or not SPARE_ONLY and not (
                        pick[1] == first_pr
                        or overdue(state["waiting"][pick[0]], when)
                        or (pick[1] == 0 and (urgent or held or main_due(
                            state["waiting"][pick[0]], when)))):
                    predicted = next_ahead(state, ahead, taken, when,
                                           busy_trees(taken, checkout, run))
            if predicted is not None:
                state.setdefault("ahead_started", {})[predicted[0]] = \
                    iso(when)
                save(state_dir, state)
            elif pick is not None:
                state["waiting"][pick[0]]["started"] = iso(when)
                save(state_dir, state)
            elif TEST_AHEAD:
                save(state_dir, state)
        starting = sum(1 for s, w in state["waiting"].items()
                       if s not in taken and just_started(w, when))
    for sha in sorted(held, key=lambda s: order(state["waiting"], s)):
        out("HELD #%d %s: %s" % (state["waiting"][sha]["pr"], sha[:10],
                                 held[sha]))
    for sha in sorted(twins, key=lambda s: order(state["waiting"], s)):
        out("HELD #%d %s: %s" % (state["waiting"][sha]["pr"], sha[:10],
                                 twins[sha]))
    if fast is not None:
        sha, pr = fast
        spawn(state_dir, sha, pr, post is not None, LANE, checkout)
        out("STARTED #%d %s in the fast lane, its steps ~%ds here; "
            "%d waiting" % (pr, sha[:10], cheap[sha], len(queued - taken)))
    if not free:
        return 0
    if predicted is not None:
        sha, pr = predicted
        spawn(state_dir, sha, pr, post is not None, free[0], checkout, True)
        out("STARTED-AHEAD #%d %s%s, the Merger's prediction; %d waiting"
            % (pr, sha[:10], "" if free[0] == 1 else " in slot %d" % free[0],
               len(queued - taken)))
        return 0
    if pick is None:
        # A start in the lane has said what this tick did.
        if fast is None and starting:
            out("STARTING: %d just started, not yet running; %d waiting"
                % (starting, len(queued - taken) - starting))
        elif fast is None and len(free) == slots and (lane_free or not lane) \
                and not held:
            out("IDLE: no open head is waiting" if not queued else
                "IDLE: %d waiting, each given up on after %d failures"
                % (len(queued), MAX_ERRORS))
        return 0
    sha, pr = pick
    if pr == 0:
        spawn(state_dir, sha, pr, post is not None, free[0], checkout,
              main=True)
        out("STARTED %s %s%s, every step%s; %d waiting"
            % (MAIN, sha[:10], "" if free[0] == 1 else " in slot %d" % free[0],
               " traced" if DEPENDS else "",
               len(queued - taken) - 1))
        return 0
    if checkout:
        spawn(state_dir, sha, pr, post is not None, free[0], checkout)
    else:
        spawn(state_dir, sha, pr, post is not None, free[0])
    out("STARTED #%d %s%s; %d waiting%s"
        % (pr, sha[:10], "" if free[0] == 1 else " in slot %d" % free[0],
           len(queued - taken) - 1,
           ", first: the Merger waits on it" if pr == first_pr else
           ", first: it has waited %d minutes"
           % (MAX_WAIT.total_seconds() // 60)
           if overdue(state["waiting"].get(sha, {}), when) else ""))
    return 0


def prepare(repo, wt, sha, pr, run=sh):
    """Check pull request pr's head out, clean and detached, at wt,
    creating that worktree of repo the first time. Returns (status,
    detail): "ready", "moved" with the head it found instead, or
    "error".

    A head already in the object store is not fetched (agent-forge
    #13: the fetch was up to 1.6 s of every run), so whether it has
    moved is only seen when it had to be fetched. A head that moved
    while it ran is stopped by the next tick (`SUPERSEDED`) either
    way."""
    if not os.path.exists(os.path.join(wt, ".git")):
        for args in (["git", "-C", repo, "worktree", "prune"],
                     ["git", "-C", repo, "worktree", "add", "-q", "--detach",
                      "--force", wt, "origin/" + MAIN]):
            got = run(args)
            if got.returncode != 0:
                return "error", "%s: %s" % (" ".join(args[3:5]),
                                            got.stderr.strip())
    if run(["git", "-C", wt, "cat-file", "-e",
            sha + "^{commit}"]).returncode != 0:
        got = run(["git", "-C", wt, "fetch", "-q", "origin",
                   "+refs/pull/%d/head" % pr])
        if got.returncode != 0:
            return "error", "fetch pull/%d/head: %s" % (pr,
                                                       got.stderr.strip())
        got = run(["git", "-C", wt, "rev-parse", "FETCH_HEAD"])
        fetched = got.stdout.strip()
        if got.returncode != 0 or len(fetched) != 40:
            return "error", "rev-parse FETCH_HEAD: %s" % got.stderr.strip()
        if fetched != sha:
            return "moved", fetched
    for args in (["git", "-C", wt, "checkout", "-q", "--detach", "--force",
                  sha],
                 ["git", "-C", wt, "clean", "-q", "-ffdx"]):
        got = run(args)
        if got.returncode != 0:
            return "error", "%s: %s" % (" ".join(args[3:5]),
                                        got.stderr.strip())
    return "ready", ""


def prepare_ahead(repo, wt, sha, run=sh):
    """Check the Merger's predicted commit sha out, clean and detached,
    at wt, as `prepare` does a head. The Merger wrote it into the
    object store wt shares with `repo`, so nothing is fetched. Returns
    ("ready", "") or ("error", why)."""
    if not os.path.exists(os.path.join(wt, ".git")):
        for args in (["git", "-C", repo, "worktree", "prune"],
                     ["git", "-C", repo, "worktree", "add", "-q", "--detach",
                      "--force", wt, "origin/" + MAIN]):
            got = run(args)
            if got.returncode != 0:
                return "error", "%s: %s" % (" ".join(args[3:5]),
                                            got.stderr.strip())
    for args in (["git", "-C", wt, "cat-file", "-e", sha + "^{commit}"],
                 ["git", "-C", wt, "checkout", "-q", "--detach", "--force",
                  sha],
                 ["git", "-C", wt, "clean", "-q", "-ffdx"]):
        got = run(args)
        if got.returncode != 0:
            return "error", "%s: %s" % (" ".join(args[3:5]),
                                        got.stderr.strip())
    return "ready", ""


def merge_default(wt, sha, pr, run=sh):
    """checks.merge_default (designs/0007): check pull request pr's head
    sha, already checked out at wt, out merged with the default
    branch's tip, as the checkout wt shares refs with last fetched it.
    The merge is `git merge-tree --write-tree` of the tip and the head,
    committed with both as parents and a fixed identity and date: the
    tree GitHub's `update-branch` makes, as the Merger predicts it.

    Returns {"base": tip, "commit": what is now checked out}, which is
    sha itself when it already contains the tip; {"base": tip,
    "conflicts": [paths]} when the merge conflicts; or {"error": why}."""
    ref = "refs/remotes/origin/" + MAIN
    got = run(["git", "-C", wt, "rev-parse", "--verify", "-q",
               ref + "^{commit}"])
    tip = (got.stdout or "").strip()
    if got.returncode != 0 or len(tip) != 40:
        return {"error": "no %s to merge with" % ref}
    if run(["git", "-C", wt, "merge-base", "--is-ancestor", tip,
            sha]).returncode == 0:
        return {"base": tip, "commit": sha}
    got = run(["git", "-C", wt, "merge-tree", "--write-tree", "--name-only",
               "--no-messages", tip, sha])
    lines = [line for line in (got.stdout or "").splitlines() if line]
    if got.returncode == 1 and lines:
        return {"base": tip, "conflicts": lines[1:]}
    if got.returncode != 0 or not lines:
        return {"error": "merge-tree with %s: %s"
                % (MAIN, (got.stderr or "").strip())}
    name, email = MERGE_IDENTITY
    env = dict(os.environ, GIT_AUTHOR_NAME=name, GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL=email,
               GIT_COMMITTER_NAME=name, GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL=email,
               GIT_AUTHOR_DATE=MERGE_DATE, GIT_COMMITTER_DATE=MERGE_DATE)
    made = run(["git", "-C", wt, "commit-tree", "--no-gpg-sign", lines[0],
                "-p", tip, "-p", sha, "-m",
                "forge-pr-check: #%d merged with %s" % (pr, MAIN)], env=env)
    commit = (made.stdout or "").strip()
    if made.returncode != 0 or len(commit) != 40:
        return {"error": "commit-tree: %s" % (made.stderr or "").strip()}
    got = run(["git", "-C", wt, "checkout", "-q", "--detach", "--force",
               commit])
    if got.returncode != 0:
        return {"error": "checkout the merge: %s" % got.stderr.strip()}
    return {"base": tip, "commit": commit}


def conflicted(state_dir, sha, pr, merge, started, finished, post,
               out=print):
    """Record and post pull request pr's head sha red, with no step
    run, because it conflicts with the default branch's tip (`merge`,
    from `merge_default`). Its author must merge the default branch
    either way."""
    with state_lock(state_dir):
        state = load(state_dir)
        seen = state["waiting"].pop(sha, {}).get("since", iso(started))
        record = {
            "pr": pr, "seen": seen, "started": iso(started),
            "finished": iso(finished), "ok": False, "failed": ["merge"],
            "seconds": 0, "step_seconds": {}, "base": merge["base"],
            "conflicts": merge["conflicts"],
        }
        state["results"][sha] = record
        save(state_dir, state)
    log(state_dir, "VERDICT #%d %s: %s conflicts with %s at %s: %s"
        % (pr, sha[:10], verdict_word(record), MAIN, merge["base"][:10],
           ", ".join(merge["conflicts"]) or "?"), finished, out)
    if post is not None:
        posted = post(sha, *final_status(record))
        with state_lock(state_dir):
            state = load(state_dir)
            if sha in state["results"]:
                state["results"][sha]["posted"] = posted
                save(state_dir, state)
    return 1


def tree_of(wt, sha, run=sh):
    """The tree sha's commit names, or None if git can't say."""
    got = run(["git", "-C", wt, "rev-parse", sha + "^{tree}"])
    tree = (got.stdout or "").strip()
    return tree if got.returncode == 0 and len(tree) == 40 else None


def flakes_of(state, sha, record):
    """The commits whose red verdict, on this host and step list, this
    green record's identical tree contradicts: a red that was not the
    tree's. Nothing for a record without a tree."""
    if not record["ok"] or not record.get("tree"):
        return []
    return sorted(other for other, r in state["results"].items()
                  if other != sha and not r.get("ok")
                  and r.get("tree") == record["tree"]
                  and r.get("host") == record["host"]
                  and r.get("steps") == record["steps"])


def matched(state_dir, sha, pr, same, tree, started, post, out=print,
            main=False):
    """Record and post green for head sha without running it: its tree
    has a green verdict here already, as `same`. Only a green verdict is
    reused; a red one is always run again."""
    with state_lock(state_dir):
        state = load(state_dir)
        source = state["results"].get(same, {})
        seen = state["waiting"].pop(sha, {}).get("since", iso(started))
        # Dated from the run it stands in for, not from now, so a match
        # of a match still ages out MATCH_MAX_AGE after the run itself.
        record = {
            "pr": pr, "seen": seen, "started": iso(started),
            "finished": source.get("finished", iso(started)), "ok": True,
            "failed": [], "seconds": 0, "tree": tree,
            "host": socket.gethostname(), "steps": steps_digest(),
            "matched": same,
            "matched_as": ("the Merger's prediction for #%s" % source.get("pr")
                           if source.get("ahead")
                           else "#%s's head" % source.get("pr")),
        }
        if source.get("selection"):
            # A match is as narrow as the run it stands in for.
            record["selection"] = source["selection"]
        if main:
            record["main"] = True
        state["results"][sha] = record
        save(state_dir, state)
    if main:
        log(state_dir, "MAIN-GREEN %s %s: matched; its tree tested green as "
            "%s %s, not run again" % (MAIN, sha[:10], record["matched_as"],
                                      same[:10]), started, out)
    else:
        log(state_dir, "MATCHED #%d %s: its tree tested green as %s %s; not "
            "run again" % (pr, sha[:10], record["matched_as"], same[:10]),
            started, out)
    if post is not None:
        posted = post(sha, *final_status(record))
        with state_lock(state_dir):
            state = load(state_dir)
            if sha in state["results"]:
                state["results"][sha]["posted"] = posted
                save(state_dir, state)
    return 0


def online_cpus():
    """The CPUs this process may run on, else the host's count."""
    try:
        return len(os.sched_getaffinity(0))
    except (AttributeError, OSError):
        return os.cpu_count() or 1


def check_cpus(online=None, slots=None):
    """CHECK_CPUS: the online CPUs shared between the slots, the fast
    lane counting as one more, and at least 1."""
    online = online_cpus() if online is None else online
    slots = slots_from_env() if slots is None else slots
    if FAST_LANE_S is not None:
        slots += 1
    return max(1, online // max(1, slots))


def step_env(state_dir, base=None, slot=1):
    """The steps' environment: `base` (os.environ) less STEP_DROP_ENV,
    every FORGE_ setting and what checks.drop_env names, with the slot's build caches (CACHES)
    outside the worktree and the slot's share of the CPUs
    (CHECK_CPUS), unless the host set them."""
    env = {k: v for k, v in (os.environ if base is None else base).items()
           if k not in STEP_DROP_ENV and k not in DROP_NAMES
           and not k.startswith((STEP_DROP_PREFIX,) + DROP_PREFIXES)}
    for name, folder in CACHES.items():
        env.setdefault(name, os.path.join(state_dir, slot_name(folder, slot)))
    env.setdefault("CHECK_CPUS", str(check_cpus()))
    # systemd-run --user finds the user's manager through it, and cron
    # doesn't set it.
    env.setdefault("XDG_RUNTIME_DIR", "/run/user/%d" % os.getuid())
    return env


def memory_max_from_env(environ=None):
    """The steps' memory limit, or None for none."""
    value = (os.environ if environ is None else environ).get(
        "FORGE_PR_CHECK_MEMORY_MAX", MEMORY_MAX).strip()
    return None if value.lower() in ("", "off") else value


def scope_prefix(memory_max, cpu_weight=None):
    """What runs a command in its own user scope under memory_max, with
    cpu_weight (default CPU_WEIGHT; 0 for systemd's own)."""
    weight = CPU_WEIGHT if cpu_weight is None else cpu_weight
    return (["systemd-run", "--user", "--scope", "--quiet", "--collect",
             "-p", "MemoryMax=" + memory_max, "-p", "MemorySwapMax=0",
             "-p", "OOMPolicy=continue"]
            + (["-p", "CPUWeight=%d" % weight] if weight else [])
            + ["--"])


def memory_cap(memory_max, env, run=sh, out=print):
    """The prefix for each step: scope_prefix(memory_max) once a scope
    is seen to start, else none, saying so."""
    if memory_max is None:
        return []
    prefix = scope_prefix(memory_max)
    got = run(prefix + ["true"], env=env, timeout=60)
    if got.returncode != 0:
        why = ((got.stderr or "") + (got.stdout or "")).strip()
        out("UNCAPPED: could not start a user scope (rc=%d%s); the steps "
            "run without a memory limit"
            % (got.returncode, ": " + why.splitlines()[-1] if why else ""))
        return []
    out("  each step limited to %s" % memory_max)
    return prefix


def tail(text, lines=TAIL_LINES):
    return "\n".join(text.rstrip("\n").splitlines()[-lines:])


def applicable(wt, steps=None, has=None):
    """The steps that apply to the head: those whose `only_if` it has,
    as a path in worktree wt, or as has(path) says."""
    if has is None:
        def has(path):
            return os.path.exists(os.path.join(wt, path))
    return [(name, args, only_if)
            for name, args, only_if in (STEPS if steps is None else steps)
            if not only_if or has(only_if)]


def applicable_at(repo, sha, run=sh):
    """`applicable` for commit sha in repo, without checking it out."""
    return applicable(repo, has=lambda path: run(
        ["git", "-C", repo, "cat-file", "-e",
         "%s:%s" % (sha, path)]).returncode == 0)


def children_cpu():
    """CPU seconds, user and system, of every child this process has
    reaped: a step's whole process tree once it has finished, since
    each process in it is waited for by its parent."""
    usage = resource.getrusage(resource.RUSAGE_CHILDREN)
    return usage.ru_utime + usage.ru_stime


def check(wt, env, run=sh, steps=None, prefix=(), wrap=None, kept=None,
          cpu=children_cpu):
    """Every step that applies to the head, each run even if an earlier
    one failed, and none retried, each after `prefix` (memory_cap).
    `wrap(name, prefix, argv, env)` gives a traced step's (argv, env).
    With `kept`, the run's results directory, each step gets an empty
    one of its own there as CHECK_RESULTS (`keep_results`). Each
    result has its wall time and its CPU time (`cpu()` before and
    after: user and system seconds of the step's process tree), so a
    step's work can be told from its waiting. Returns (ok, results)."""
    results = []
    for name, args, only_if in applicable(wt, steps):
        step_env_, folder = env, None
        if kept is not None:
            folder = os.path.join(kept, results_name(name))
            shutil.rmtree(folder, ignore_errors=True)
            os.makedirs(folder)
            step_env_ = dict(env, CHECK_RESULTS=folder)
        step_args = list(prefix) + args
        if wrap is not None:
            step_args, step_env_ = wrap(name, list(prefix), args, step_env_)
        started, used = time.monotonic(), cpu()
        got = run(step_args, cwd=wt, env=step_env_,
                  timeout=STEP_TIMEOUT_S)
        wall = time.monotonic() - started
        result = {"name": name, "rc": got.returncode,
                  "seconds": int(wall), "wall": round(wall, 2),
                  "cpu": round(max(0.0, cpu() - used), 2),
                  "tail": tail((got.stdout or "") + (got.stderr or ""))}
        if folder is not None:
            result["results_bytes"], dropped = keep_results(folder)
            if dropped is not None:
                result["results_dropped"] = dropped
        results.append(result)
    return all(r["rc"] == 0 for r in results), results


def results_name(step):
    """A step's results directory's name: its name, safe as one."""
    return re.sub(r"[^A-Za-z0-9._-]", "_", step).lstrip(".") or "_"


def tree_size(path):
    """The bytes of the files under path, never following a link."""
    total = 0
    for top, dirs, files in os.walk(path):
        for name in files:
            try:
                total += os.lstat(os.path.join(top, name)).st_size
            except OSError:
                pass
    return total


def keep_results(folder):
    """(bytes kept, bytes dropped or None) for a step's results
    directory once it has run: over RESULTS_MAX, what it left is
    dropped whole, since part of a record could mislead; empty, the
    directory goes."""
    size = tree_size(folder)
    if size > RESULTS_MAX:
        shutil.rmtree(folder, ignore_errors=True)
        return 0, size
    if size == 0:
        shutil.rmtree(folder, ignore_errors=True)
    return size, None


def files_under(root):
    """(path, lstat) for every file under root, never following a link:
    a link is neither counted nor removed, and nothing outside root is
    reached."""
    for top, dirs, files in os.walk(root):
        for name in files:
            path = os.path.join(top, name)
            try:
                st = os.lstat(path)
            except OSError:
                continue
            if not os.path.islink(path):
                yield path, st


def newest_mtime(path):
    """The newest mtime of path and of anything under it."""
    newest = os.lstat(path).st_mtime
    for top, dirs, files in os.walk(path):
        for name in dirs + files:
            try:
                newest = max(newest, os.lstat(os.path.join(top, name)).st_mtime)
            except OSError:
                pass
    return newest


def prune_caches(roots, limit, now, main_since=None):
    """Prune a slot's build caches, `roots`, in three passes, and return
    what went: {"executables", "incremental", "units", "freed", "left"}.

    1. Executables directly in any `deps` directory older than
       STALE_EXECUTABLE that the default branch's last green run in
       the slot didn't write (mtime before `main_since`, its start):
       each head's rebuilt test binaries, which nothing runs again.
    2. Directories directly in any `incremental` directory with nothing
       in them touched for STALE_INCREMENTAL.
    3. Then, until the caches hold at most `limit`, whole directories,
       each aged by its newest file, oldest first: those directly in an
       `incremental` directory, then those directly in a root. Never a
       single file: a build tool trusts what it recorded about the
       files it wrote, so one missing from a directory it still uses
       can break a build (a hollow incremental session crashed rustc),
       where a missing directory only makes it start that part afresh.

    `now` and `main_since` are epoch seconds. A root that is a link, or
    isn't there, is left alone."""
    gone = {"executables": 0, "incremental": 0, "units": 0, "freed": 0}
    roots = [r for r in roots if os.path.isdir(r) and not os.path.islink(r)]
    stale = now - STALE_EXECUTABLE.total_seconds()
    if main_since is not None:
        stale = min(stale, main_since)
    old = now - STALE_INCREMENTAL.total_seconds()

    def remove(path, size, kind):
        try:
            os.unlink(path)
        except OSError:
            return
        gone[kind] += 1
        gone["freed"] += size

    for root in roots:
        for top, dirs, files in os.walk(root):
            name = os.path.basename(top)
            if name == "deps":
                for entry in files:
                    path = os.path.join(top, entry)
                    try:
                        st = os.lstat(path)
                    except OSError:
                        continue
                    if (not os.path.islink(path) and st.st_mode & 0o111
                            and st.st_mtime < stale):
                        remove(path, st.st_size, "executables")
            elif name == "incremental":
                for entry in list(dirs):
                    path = os.path.join(top, entry)
                    try:
                        if os.path.islink(path) or newest_mtime(path) >= old:
                            continue
                        size = sum(st.st_size for _, st in files_under(path))
                        shutil.rmtree(path)
                    except OSError:
                        continue
                    dirs.remove(entry)
                    gone["incremental"] += 1
                    gone["freed"] += size
    total = sum(st.st_size for root in roots for _, st in files_under(root))
    if limit is not None:
        for units in (lambda: incremental_sessions(roots),
                      lambda: top_directories(roots)):
            if total <= limit:
                break
            for path in sorted(units(), key=newest_mtime):
                if total <= limit:
                    break
                size = sum(st.st_size for _, st in files_under(path))
                try:
                    shutil.rmtree(path)
                except OSError:
                    continue
                gone["units"] += 1
                gone["freed"] += size
                total -= size
    gone["left"] = total
    return gone


def incremental_sessions(roots):
    """Every directory directly in an `incremental` directory under
    roots: one crate's incremental state, which is used whole or not
    at all."""
    found = []
    for root in roots:
        for top, dirs, files in os.walk(root):
            if os.path.basename(top) == "incremental":
                found += [os.path.join(top, d) for d in dirs
                          if not os.path.islink(os.path.join(top, d))]
                dirs.clear()
    return found


def top_directories(roots):
    """Every directory directly in a root: a build profile, or a target
    with its profiles."""
    found = []
    for root in roots:
        for entry in os.scandir(root):
            if entry.is_dir(follow_symlinks=False):
                found.append(entry.path)
    return found


def prune_slot_caches(state_dir, slot, out=print):
    """With CACHE_LIMIT, prune the slot's caches after a run, and log
    what went as CACHE-PRUNED. Never raises: a failure is logged, and
    the run's verdict stands."""
    if CACHE_LIMIT is None or not CACHES:
        return None
    try:
        since = load(state_dir).get("cache_main", {}).get(str(slot))
        gone = prune_caches(
            [os.path.join(state_dir, slot_name(folder, slot))
             for folder in CACHES.values()],
            CACHE_LIMIT, time.time(), since)
    except Exception as err:
        log(state_dir, "CACHE-PRUNE-FAILED %s: %s: %s"
            % (slot_label(slot), type(err).__name__, err), now_utc(), out)
        return None
    if gone["freed"]:
        log(state_dir, "CACHE-PRUNED %s: freed %d bytes (%d executables, %d "
            "incremental directories, %d directories over the limit); %d "
            "bytes left" % (slot_label(slot), gone["freed"],
                            gone["executables"], gone["incremental"],
                            gone["units"], gone["left"]),
            now_utc(), out)
    return gone


def prune_results(state_dir, state, when):
    """Each results directory whose head has no verdict in `state`,
    once it is RESULTS_ORPHAN_AGE old: its verdict was pruned (KEEP),
    or never came."""
    top = os.path.join(state_dir, RESULTS_DIR)
    try:
        names = os.listdir(top)
    except OSError:
        return
    cutoff = (when - RESULTS_ORPHAN_AGE).timestamp()
    for name in names:
        path = os.path.join(top, name)
        try:
            old = os.lstat(path).st_mtime < cutoff
        except OSError:
            continue
        if name not in state["results"] and old:
            shutil.rmtree(path, ignore_errors=True)


def choose(wt, sha, state_dir, run=sh, names=None):
    """The steps head `sha` needs, from the newest dependency record
    that answers for its merge base (checks.depends): {"base", "record"
    (the recorded commit), "depends" (the record's path), "ran" ({step:
    why}), "skipped": [steps]}, or {"every": why} when every step must
    run (see the module docstring).

    wt is the head's worktree, or, with `names` (the steps that apply
    to it), any checkout that has the commit and the default branch's
    remote ref, such as the cron checkout: nothing here reads files."""
    if names is None:
        names = [name for name, _, _ in applicable(wt)]
    ref = "refs/remotes/origin/" + MAIN
    got = run(["git", "-C", wt, "merge-base", sha, ref])
    base = (got.stdout or "").strip()
    if got.returncode != 0 or len(base) != 40:
        return {"every": "no merge base with %s" % MAIN}
    digest = steps_digest()
    records = [(path, r) for path, r in trace.load_records(state_dir)
               if r.get("steps") == digest]
    if not records:
        return {"every": "no dependency record for these steps"}
    tree = tree_of(wt, base, run)
    chosen = next(((path, r) for path, r in records
                   if tree and r.get("tree") == tree), None)
    if chosen is None:
        for path, r in records:
            if run(["git", "-C", wt, "merge-base", "--is-ancestor",
                    r["commit"], base]).returncode == 0:
                chosen = (path, r)
                break
    if chosen is None:
        return {"every": "no dependency record for %s or before it"
                % base[:10]}
    path, record = chosen
    changed, touched = set(), set()
    pairs = [(base, sha)]
    if record.get("tree") != tree:
        # What changed between the record and the base counts too.
        pairs.append((record["commit"], base))
    for old, new in pairs:
        got = run(["git", "-C", wt, "diff", "--name-status", "--no-renames",
                   old, new])
        if got.returncode != 0:
            return {"every": "could not list what changed since %s"
                    % old[:10]}
        more, added = trace.changes(got.stdout)
        changed |= more
        touched |= added
    ran, skipped = trace.select(names, ALWAYS, record, changed, touched)
    return {"base": base, "record": record["commit"], "depends": path,
            "ran": ran, "skipped": skipped}


# (wt, old, new) -> (changed, touched) or None: a diff between two
# commits or trees never changes, so a tick or a run works it out once.
_DIFFS = {}


def diff_of(wt, old, new, run=sh):
    """(changed, touched) between two commits or trees (trace.changes),
    or None if git can't say, such as when one has been collected."""
    key = (wt, old, new)
    if key not in _DIFFS:
        got = run(["git", "-C", wt, "diff", "--name-status", "--no-renames",
                   old, new])
        _DIFFS[key] = trace.changes(got.stdout) if got.returncode == 0 \
            else None
    return _DIFFS[key]


def choose_fresh(wt, sha, state_dir, run=sh, names=None, onto=None):
    """`choose` with checks.fresh (designs/0010): each step from its own
    record. As `choose` returns, plus "records" ({step: the record's
    path}) and "stale" (the steps that run only because their record
    is stale). "record" and "depends" are the newest record chosen.

    The base is the merge base with the default branch, or `onto` (the
    default branch's tip, for an estimate with MERGE_DEFAULT, where the
    head will be checked merged with it). The head's own changes are
    from its merge base.

    A record answers for a step at the base when nothing the step read
    or listed changed between the record's tree and the base's: a step
    reads what its inputs lead it to, so it reads the same at both.
    That holds for any record, an ancestor or not, so the candidates
    are the records of the base's tree and the newest FRESH_CANDIDATES
    others; each step is chosen from the first, of those with the
    step, that answers for it (the base's tree first, then newest). A
    step none answers for is stale: it is chosen from the first that
    has it, and runs, for its own changes or else because it is stale.
    A step no record has runs."""
    if names is None:
        names = [name for name, _, _ in applicable(wt)]
    ref = "refs/remotes/origin/" + MAIN
    got = run(["git", "-C", wt, "merge-base", sha, ref])
    merged = (got.stdout or "").strip()
    if got.returncode != 0 or len(merged) != 40:
        return {"every": "no merge base with %s" % MAIN}
    base = onto or merged
    digest = steps_digest()
    records = [(path, r) for path, r in trace.load_records(state_dir)
               if r.get("steps") == digest]
    if not records:
        return {"every": "no dependency record for these steps"}
    tree = tree_of(wt, base, run)
    if tree is None:
        return {"every": "could not read %s's tree" % base[:10]}
    own = diff_of(wt, merged, sha, run)
    if own is None:
        return {"every": "could not list what changed since %s" % merged[:10]}
    exact = [(p, r) for p, r in records if r.get("tree") == tree]
    others = [(p, r) for p, r in records if r.get("tree") != tree]
    candidates = [(p, r, (set(), set())) for p, r in exact]
    for path, record in others[:FRESH_CANDIDATES]:
        since = diff_of(wt, record.get("tree") or record["commit"], tree, run)
        if since is not None:
            candidates.append((path, record, since))
    if not candidates:
        return {"every": "could not list what changed since any record"}
    ran, skipped, stale, chosen = {}, [], [], {}
    for name in names:
        having = [c for c in candidates if name in c[1]["reads"]]
        answers = next((c for c in having
                        if trace.why_runs(name, c[1], *c[2]) is None), None)
        path, record, since = answers or (having[0] if having else
                                          (None, None, None))
        if path is not None:
            chosen[name] = path
        if name in ALWAYS:
            ran[name] = "always"
        elif record is None:
            ran[name] = "not in any record"
        else:
            why = trace.why_runs(name, record, *own)
            if why is None and answers is None:
                why = "%s since its record" % \
                    trace.why_runs(name, record, *since)
                stale.append(name)
            if why is None:
                skipped.append(name)
            else:
                ran[name] = why
    used = set(chosen.values())
    path, record = next(((p, r) for p, r, _ in candidates if p in used),
                        candidates[0][:2])
    return {"base": base, "record": record["commit"], "depends": path,
            "ran": ran, "skipped": skipped, "records": chosen,
            "stale": stale}


def compose(state_dir, selection, slot, when):
    """The record a fresh selection chose each step from, as one record
    ({"reads", "dirs", "from": {step: path}}), written as
    depends/.selection-<slot>.json for CHECK_DEPENDS and the steps'
    digests: (path, record). A step whose record has been pruned since
    is left out, as a step no record has."""
    loaded, reads, dirs = {}, {}, {}
    for name, path in sorted(selection["records"].items()):
        if path not in loaded:
            try:
                with open(path) as handle:
                    loaded[path] = json.load(handle)
            except (OSError, ValueError):
                loaded[path] = None
        record = loaded[path]
        if not isinstance(record, dict) or \
                name not in (record.get("reads") or {}):
            continue
        reads[name] = record["reads"][name]
        dirs[name] = (record.get("dirs") or {}).get(name, [])
    sources = {name: selection["records"][name] for name in reads}
    record = {"reads": reads, "dirs": dirs, "from": sources}
    path = trace.write_record(state_dir, "", selection["record"],
                              steps_digest(), iso(when), "", reads, dirs,
                              source="selection", sources=sources,
                              name=".selection-%d.json" % slot)
    return path, record


def records_key(state_dir, tip):
    """What a fresh estimate depends on besides the head: the default
    branch's tip and the records there are. A head is estimated again
    when it changes."""
    folder = trace.records_dir(state_dir)
    try:
        names = sorted(n for n in os.listdir(folder)
                       if n.endswith(".json") and not n.startswith("."))
        stamps = [os.stat(os.path.join(folder, n)).st_mtime_ns
                  for n in names]
    except OSError:
        names, stamps = [], []
    return "%s %d %d" % (tip or "", len(names), max(stamps or [0]))


def main_needs(checkout, head, state_dir, run=sh):
    """The steps the default branch's head (sha, tree) needs traced
    (checks.fresh): those whose record is stale at its tree, or that
    no record has. None means every step (no record answers at all).
    A head that needs none isn't traced: every record still holds."""
    sha, _ = head
    names = [name for name, _, _ in applicable_at(checkout, sha, run)]
    selection = choose_fresh(checkout, sha, state_dir, run, names)
    if "every" in selection:
        return None
    return sorted(n for n, why in selection["ran"].items()
                  if n in selection["stale"] or why == "not in any record")


def busy_trees(shas, checkout, run=sh):
    """The trees of the commits slots are checking now (`shas`), those
    the checkout has."""
    if not checkout:
        return frozenset()
    trees = (tree_of(checkout, sha, run) for sha in shas)
    return frozenset(t for t in trees if t)


def twin_heads(state, running, checkout, run=sh):
    """{sha: why} of the waiting heads whose tree a slot is checking now
    as the Merger's prediction for the same pull request (`running`:
    holder_fields of each slot). Run beside it, a head would only check
    the same tree twice; held until the prediction's verdict is in, it
    is matched to it at once (or, if that is red, is no worse off)."""
    if not checkout:
        return {}
    predictions = [(f[0], f[1]) for f in running if f and f[4] and f[0]]
    if not predictions:
        return {}
    trees = {}

    def tree(sha):
        if sha not in trees:
            trees[sha] = tree_of(checkout, sha, run)
        return trees[sha]
    twins = {}
    for sha, entry in state["waiting"].items():
        if entry.get("main"):
            continue
        for pr, predicted in predictions:
            if entry.get("pr") == pr and tree(sha) is not None \
                    and tree(sha) == tree(predicted):
                twins[sha] = ("its tree is being checked now as the "
                              "Merger's prediction %s; waits to be "
                              "matched to it" % predicted[:10])
                break
    return twins


def held_heads(state, tip, busy, when, first_pr=None, cheap=()):
    """{sha: why} of the waiting heads that wait for a fresh record
    (checks.fresh): whose estimate, against the default branch's tip,
    runs a step only because its record is stale, while the tip waits
    for its traced run or is running it, for at most FRESH_WAIT from
    when the head was first seen. Not the Merger's head, nor a cheap
    one, which run now."""
    if FRESH_WAIT is None or tip is None:
        return {}
    coming = tip in busy or (state["waiting"].get(tip) or {}).get("main")
    if not coming:
        return {}
    held = {}
    for sha, entry in state["waiting"].items():
        stale = entry.get("stale_steps")
        if not stale or entry.get("main") or sha in cheap \
                or entry.get("lane_base") != tip \
                or (first_pr is not None and entry.get("pr") == first_pr):
            continue
        try:
            waited = when - parse(entry["since"])
        except (KeyError, TypeError, ValueError):
            continue
        if waited < FRESH_WAIT:
            held[sha] = "%s stale; waits for %s %s's record, at most %dm" \
                % (", ".join(stale), MAIN, tip[:10],
                   FRESH_WAIT.total_seconds() // 60)
    return held


def step_costs(state):
    """{step: seconds}, each step's cost here: the median of its last
    COST_RUNS runs in the verdicts kept, newest by `finished`. A step
    with fewer than COST_MIN_RUNS runs is left out, so it counts as
    expensive."""
    runs = {}
    for r in sorted(state["results"].values(),
                    key=lambda r: str(r.get("finished", ""))):
        seconds = r.get("step_seconds")
        if not isinstance(seconds, dict):
            continue
        for name, took in seconds.items():
            if isinstance(took, (int, float)) and not isinstance(took, bool):
                runs.setdefault(name, []).append(took)
    return {name: statistics.median(took[-COST_RUNS:])
            for name, took in runs.items() if len(took) >= COST_MIN_RUNS}


def lane_steps(checkout, sha, pr, state_dir, run=sh, tip=None):
    """The steps pull request pr's head sha would run (`choose`, or
    `choose_fresh` with checks.fresh, against `tip` with MERGE_DEFAULT),
    worked out in the cron checkout without a worktree, fetching the
    head first if the checkout doesn't have it. The fetch writes no
    FETCH_HEAD, which the checkout's other users may read. As `choose`
    returns, with {"every": why} when the steps can't be worked out."""
    def have():
        return run(["git", "-C", checkout, "cat-file", "-e",
                    sha + "^{commit}"]).returncode == 0
    if not have():
        got = run(["git", "-C", checkout, "fetch", "-q",
                   "--no-write-fetch-head", "origin",
                   "refs/pull/%d/head" % pr])
        if got.returncode != 0:
            return {"every": "could not fetch pull/%d/head: %s"
                    % (pr, (got.stderr or "").strip())}
        if not have():
            return {"every": "pull/%d/head is no longer %s" % (pr, sha[:10])}
    names = [name for name, _, _ in applicable_at(checkout, sha, run)]
    if FRESH_WAIT is not None:
        return choose_fresh(checkout, sha, state_dir, run, names,
                            tip if MERGE_DEFAULT else None)
    return choose(checkout, sha, state_dir, run, names)


def estimate(state_dir, checkout, busy=(), run=sh, tip=None):
    """Work out, in the cron checkout, the steps each waiting pull
    request head would run that has no estimate yet (checks.fast_lane),
    and keep them with the head as `lane_steps`. Only an answer is
    kept: a head whose steps can't be worked out yet is tried again
    next tick. A head being run, or given up on, needs none.

    With checks.fresh, `tip` is the default branch's tip, and a head is
    estimated again whenever it or the records change (`records_key`);
    the steps that would run only because their record is stale are
    kept as `stale_steps`, and the base they were chosen against as
    `lane_base`."""
    key = records_key(state_dir, tip) if FRESH_WAIT is not None else None
    with state_lock(state_dir):
        waiting = load(state_dir)["waiting"]
        todo = sorted((w["since"], sha, w["pr"]) for sha, w in waiting.items()
                      if ("lane_steps" not in w if key is None
                          else w.get("lane_key") != key)
                      and not w.get("main")
                      and sha not in busy
                      and w.get("errors", 0) < MAX_ERRORS
                      and w.get("faults", 0) < MAX_ERRORS)
    found = {}
    for _, sha, pr in todo:
        selection = lane_steps(checkout, sha, pr, state_dir, run, tip) \
            if key is not None else \
            lane_steps(checkout, sha, pr, state_dir, run)
        if "ran" in selection:
            found[sha] = {"lane_steps": sorted(selection["ran"])}
            if key is not None:
                found[sha].update(stale_steps=selection["stale"],
                                  lane_base=selection["base"], lane_key=key)
    if found:
        with state_lock(state_dir):
            state = load(state_dir)
            for sha, got in found.items():
                if sha in state["waiting"]:
                    state["waiting"][sha].update(got)
            save(state_dir, state)


def lane_cost(entry, costs):
    """A waiting head's estimated seconds, the sum of its chosen steps'
    costs, or None when it has no estimate or a step has no cost."""
    steps = entry.get("lane_steps")
    if not isinstance(steps, list) or entry.get("main") \
            or any(name not in costs for name in steps):
        return None
    return sum(costs[name] for name in steps)


def cheap_heads(state, costs=None):
    """{sha: estimated seconds} of the waiting heads that are cheap: whose
    chosen steps have together taken at most FAST_LANE_S here."""
    if FAST_LANE_S is None:
        return {}
    costs = step_costs(state) if costs is None else costs
    cheap = {}
    for sha, entry in state["waiting"].items():
        cost = lane_cost(entry, costs)
        if cost is not None and cost <= FAST_LANE_S:
            cheap[sha] = cost
    return cheap


def covers(selection, older=None):
    """accept(record) for `tested`: whether an earlier verdict ran every
    step this head would, under the same base or an older one. A verdict
    that ran every step covers any head; a narrower one only a head that
    needs no more of them, compared with the same base, or with one
    `older(its base, this head's)` says is an ancestor of this head's.

    Same tree, older base: the earlier run counted everything since its
    base as changed, which takes in all this head counts, so its steps,
    and what each narrowed to by CHECK_BASE, were a superset. That is
    the Merger's second prediction and on: predicted on top of the
    prediction before it, it was run against the default branch before
    that merged, while the head the Merger updates later, with the same
    tree, is based on the merge itself."""
    def accept(record):
        earlier = record.get("selection") or {}
        if "ran" not in earlier:
            return True
        if "ran" not in (selection or {}):
            return False
        if not set(selection["ran"]) <= set(earlier["ran"]):
            return False
        base, theirs = selection["base"], earlier.get("base")
        return theirs == base or (
            older is not None and isinstance(theirs, str)
            and older(theirs, base))
    return accept


def tree_entries(wt, commit, run=sh):
    """({path: "mode type oid"} for every file and directory in commit's
    tree, {directory: sorted entry names}), or None if git can't say.
    "." is the top."""
    got = run(["git", "-C", wt, "ls-tree", "-r", "-t", "-z", "--full-tree",
               commit])
    if got.returncode != 0:
        return None
    entries, children = {}, {}
    for item in (got.stdout or "").split("\0"):
        meta, _, path = item.partition("\t")
        if not path:
            continue
        entries[path] = meta
        parent, _, name = path.rpartition("/")
        children.setdefault(parent or ".", []).append(name)
    return entries, {d: sorted(names) for d, names in children.items()}


def step_digests(wt, commit, record, env, run=sh, names=None):
    """{step: input digest} (designs/0009) for the steps that apply to
    the worktree, at `commit` (the head, or its merge with the default
    branch), from dependency record `record`. A step gets none when the
    record doesn't know it, its `version` command fails, or it read a
    path an earlier step may have written and an earlier step has none.

    A digest covers what the step read, at commit: each path's mode and
    blob (or "absent": a path it looked for, or one a step wrote), the
    names each directory it listed holds, CHECK_BASE's tree when the
    step is given one (unless it says `"reads_base": false`), its
    configuration entry, and its `version`
    command's output. The worktree is cleaned before any step runs, so
    a path the step read that commit doesn't have was either not there
    or written by a step; when there is one, the digests of every
    earlier step count too, since which step wrote it isn't recorded."""
    if names is None:
        names = [name for name, _, _ in applicable(wt)]
    listed = tree_entries(wt, commit, run)
    if listed is None:
        return {}
    entries, children = listed
    base = env.get("CHECK_BASE")
    base_tree = tree_of(wt, base, run) if base else None
    if base and base_tree is None:
        return {}
    steps = {name: (name, args, only_if) for name, args, only_if in STEPS}
    digests, earlier = {}, []
    # Each distinct version command runs once: steps usually share one,
    # and on a loaded host a run is a quarter of a second.
    versions = {}
    for name in names:
        reads = record.get("reads", {}).get(name)
        if reads is None:
            earlier.append(None)
            continue
        h = hashlib.sha256()

        def add(*parts):
            h.update(json.dumps(parts).encode() + b"\n")
        add("entry", STEP_ENTRIES.get(name) or list(steps.get(name, ())))
        written = False
        for path in sorted(reads):
            meta = entries.get(path)
            if meta is None and path != ".":
                written = True
            add("read", path, meta or "absent")
        for folder in sorted(record.get("dirs", {}).get(name, ())):
            add("dir", folder, children.get(folder))
        if name not in NO_BASE:
            add("base", base_tree)
        if written:
            if None in earlier:
                earlier.append(None)
                continue
            add("earlier", earlier)
        version = VERSIONS.get(name)
        if version:
            key = tuple(version)
            if key not in versions:
                got = run(version, cwd=wt, env=env,
                          timeout=VERSION_TIMEOUT_S)
                versions[key] = (got.stdout or "") \
                    if got.returncode == 0 else None
            if versions[key] is None:
                earlier.append(None)
                continue
            add("version", versions[key])
        digests[name] = h.hexdigest()
        earlier.append(digests[name])
    return digests


def reusable(state, name, digest, when):
    """What step `name`'s verdict for these inputs can be taken from:
    {"reused": sha, "pr", "finished"} of the run that ran it green, or
    None. That is the newest verdict here (this host, these steps) with
    the same digest for the step that ran it or reused it; a red one, or
    one older than REUSE_AGE, gives nothing. A reuse points at the run
    it reuses, and ages from it."""
    host, steps = socket.gethostname(), steps_digest()
    found = []
    for sha, r in state["results"].items():
        if (r.get("digests") or {}).get(name) != digest \
                or r.get("host") != host or r.get("steps") != steps:
            continue
        if name in (r.get("reused") or {}):
            found.append((r.get("finished", ""), r["reused"][name]))
        elif name in (r.get("step_seconds") or {}):
            green = name not in r.get("failed", [])
            found.append((r.get("finished", ""), {
                "reused": sha, "pr": r.get("pr"),
                "finished": r.get("finished")} if green else None))
    if not found:
        return None
    _, origin = max(found, key=lambda f: f[0])
    if origin is None:
        return None
    try:
        if when - parse(origin["finished"]) > REUSE_AGE:
            return None
    except (KeyError, TypeError, ValueError):
        return None
    return dict(origin)


def reuse_steps(state_dir, digests, names, when):
    """{step: what it reuses} for those of `names` whose verdict can be
    reused (`reusable`); never a step marked always."""
    with state_lock(state_dir):
        state = load(state_dir)
    found = {}
    for name in names:
        if name in ALWAYS or name not in digests:
            continue
        origin = reusable(state, name, digests[name], when)
        if origin is not None:
            found[name] = origin
    return found


def reuse_text(name, origin):
    """"test as #7 at 1a2b3c4": where a reused step's verdict is from."""
    pr = origin.get("pr")
    return "%s as %s at %s" % (name, "#%s" % pr if pr else MAIN,
                               (origin.get("reused") or "")[:7])


def tracer_for(state_dir, run=sh):
    """("preload", library) or ("strace", None) for checks.tracer, or
    None when it can't be used: no strace, or no library built."""
    if TRACER == "strace":
        return ("strace", None) if trace.have_strace() else None
    lib = trace.build(os.path.join(state_dir, "tracer"), run)
    return ("preload", lib) if lib else None


def trace_wrapper(state_dir, slot, tracer, files):
    """wrap() for `check`: each step traced into its own file, noted in
    `files` ({step: path})."""
    kind, lib = tracer
    folder = trace.records_dir(state_dir)
    os.makedirs(folder, exist_ok=True)

    def wrap(name, prefix, args, env):
        path = os.path.join(folder, ".trace-%d-%d" % (slot, len(files)))
        try:
            os.unlink(path)
        except OSError:
            pass
        files[name] = path
        if kind == "strace":
            return prefix + trace.strace_prefix(path) + args, env
        return prefix + args, trace.preload_env(env, lib, path)
    return wrap


def record_depends(state_dir, wt, sha, tree, files, env, tracer, when,
                   out=print):
    """Writes the dependency record of a green traced run, or says why
    not: a step whose trace is implausible (it recorded nothing, not
    even its own program) makes the record unusable. Returns its path,
    or None. The trace files are removed either way."""
    kind, _ = tracer
    programs = {name: args[0] for name, args, _ in applicable(wt)}
    inside = [os.path.relpath(env[v], wt) for v in CACHES if v in env
              and os.path.abspath(env[v]).startswith(
                  os.path.abspath(wt) + os.sep)]
    reads, dirs, implausible = {}, {}, []
    for name, path in files.items():
        entries = trace.read_strace(path, wt) if kind == "strace" \
            else trace.read_preload(path)
        try:
            os.unlink(path)
        except OSError:
            pass
        if not trace.plausible(entries, programs.get(name, ""),
                               env.get("PATH")):
            implausible.append(name)
            continue
        reads[name] = trace.relative(entries, wt, inside)
        dirs[name] = trace.directories(reads[name], wt)
    if implausible:
        log(state_dir, "DEPENDS-UNUSABLE %s %s: %s recorded nothing, not "
            "even its own program; nothing recorded"
            % (MAIN, sha[:10], ", ".join(implausible)), when, out)
        return None
    if not tree:
        return None
    path = trace.write_record(state_dir, tree, sha, steps_digest(),
                              iso(when), kind, reads, dirs)
    trace.prune_records(state_dir, when, parse,
                        FRESH_KEEP if FRESH_WAIT is not None else None)
    out("  recorded what %d steps read: %s"
        % (len(reads), os.path.basename(path)))
    return path


def record_run(state_dir, wt, sha, checked, tree, files, env, tracer, when,
               selection, composed, name, out=print):
    """With checks.fresh, writes the dependency record of a pull
    request's green traced run (designs/0010), for the tree it checked
    (`checked`: the head, or its merge with the default branch), and
    returns its path, or None. Only steps that say `"reads_base":
    false` are recorded: one given CHECK_BASE may narrow its run to
    what changed since the base, so its trace isn't what a full run
    reads. Of those, a step that ran gives what its trace read, and a
    skipped one what its chosen record (`composed`) says, which still
    holds: it read nothing that changed. A step reused (designs/0009)
    or given no record gives nothing, so the record may lack steps.

    A step whose trace is implausible makes the whole record unusable,
    as for the default branch. A record of the default branch's own
    for this tree is never replaced; one of another run is added to,
    its steps kept. The trace files are removed either way."""
    kind, _ = tracer
    programs = {step: args[0] for step, args, _ in applicable(wt)}
    inside = [os.path.relpath(env[v], wt) for v in CACHES if v in env
              and os.path.abspath(env[v]).startswith(
                  os.path.abspath(wt) + os.sep)]
    reads, dirs, sources, implausible = {}, {}, {}, []
    for step, path in files.items():
        entries = None
        if step in NO_BASE:
            entries = trace.read_strace(path, wt) if kind == "strace" \
                else trace.read_preload(path)
        try:
            os.unlink(path)
        except OSError:
            pass
        if entries is None:
            continue
        if not trace.plausible(entries, programs.get(step, ""),
                               env.get("PATH")):
            implausible.append(step)
            continue
        reads[step] = trace.relative(entries, wt, inside)
        dirs[step] = trace.directories(reads[step], wt)
        sources[step] = "run"
    if implausible:
        log(state_dir, "DEPENDS-UNUSABLE %s %s: %s recorded nothing, not "
            "even its own program; nothing recorded"
            % (name, sha[:10], ", ".join(sorted(implausible))), when, out)
        return None
    skipped = (selection or {}).get("skipped", ())
    for step in skipped:
        if step in NO_BASE and composed and step in composed["reads"]:
            reads[step] = composed["reads"][step]
            dirs[step] = composed["dirs"].get(step, [])
            sources[step] = composed["from"][step]
    if not reads or not tree:
        return None
    path = os.path.join(trace.records_dir(state_dir), tree + ".json")
    try:
        with open(path) as handle:
            earlier = json.load(handle)
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        earlier = None
    if isinstance(earlier, dict) and earlier.get("steps") == steps_digest() \
            and isinstance(earlier.get("reads"), dict):
        if earlier.get("source") is None:
            # The default branch's own traced run: every step, in full.
            return None
        for step, read in earlier["reads"].items():
            if step not in reads:
                reads[step] = read
                dirs[step] = (earlier.get("dirs") or {}).get(step, [])
                sources[step] = (earlier.get("from") or {}).get(step, "run")
    path = trace.write_record(state_dir, tree, checked, steps_digest(),
                              iso(when), kind, reads, dirs, source="run",
                              sources=sources)
    trace.prune_records(state_dir, when, parse, FRESH_KEEP)
    out("  recorded what %d steps read: %s"
        % (len(reads), os.path.basename(path)))
    return path


def start_next_tick(popen=None, checkout=None, state_dir=None,
                    merger_dir=None):
    """Start one `forge-pr-check --post` tick through forge-cron-run,
    detached, and return its argv. Not waited for: the caller is about
    to exit. The tick uses the checkout this worker checked from, and
    the `--state-dir` and `--merger-dir` the cron line gave, if it gave
    them: a chained tick on the default directories would be a second
    queue posting to the same status context."""
    popen = popen or subprocess.Popen
    argv = [CRON_RUN] + list(NEXT_TICK) \
        + (["--state-dir", state_dir] if state_dir else []) \
        + (["--merger-dir", merger_dir] if merger_dir else [])
    env = dict(os.environ)
    if checkout:
        env["FORGE_CRON_CHECKOUT"] = checkout
    popen(argv, env=env, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL,
          stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, start_new_session=True, close_fds=True)
    return argv


class Phases:
    """Where a run's wall time goes outside its steps (agent-forge #13):
    seconds by phase, each mark charging the time since the last one."""

    def __init__(self, timer=time.monotonic):
        self.timer = timer
        self.last = timer()
        self.spent = {}

    def mark(self, phase):
        now = self.timer()
        self.spent[phase] = round(self.spent.get(phase, 0) + now - self.last,
                                  2)
        self.last = now

    def text(self):
        return ", ".join("%s %.1fs" % kv for kv in self.spent.items())


def worker(repo, state_dir, sha, pr, run=sh, out=print, clock=now_utc,
           post=None, chain=None, slot=1, memory_max=None, ahead=False,
           main=False, report=None):
    """Check sha, pull request pr's head, now, in this process, in
    `slot`, each step limited to memory_max (None: no limit). With
    `ahead`, sha is the Merger's prediction for pr: checked out without
    a fetch, recorded with `ahead`, and posted nowhere. With TEST_AHEAD,
    a head whose tree is green here already is posted green unrun.

    With `main`, sha is the default branch's head (checks.depends): run
    in full under the tracer, logged MAIN-GREEN or MAIN-RED, posted on
    that commit, and when green its dependency record written. With
    DEPENDS, a pull request's head runs only the steps that read what
    it changes. With MERGE_DEFAULT, a pull request's head is checked
    merged with the default branch's tip (`merge_default`).

    Where the run's own time went, outside its steps, is logged as
    `overhead:` and kept in the verdict as `overhead`, seconds by phase
    (`Phases`; agent-forge #13). Each step's own time is kept twice:
    `step_seconds`, whole seconds, and `step_wall`, to the hundredth;
    and its CPU time, user and system seconds of its process tree, as
    `step_cpu`."""
    os.makedirs(state_dir, exist_ok=True)
    phases = Phases()
    started = clock()
    # The wall clock, which the caches' mtimes are read against.
    begun = time.time()
    held = try_lock(lock_path(state_dir, slot),
                    "#%d %s %s pid=%d%s" % (pr, sha, iso(started),
                                            os.getpid(),
                                            " ahead" if ahead else
                                            " main" if main else ""))
    if held is None:
        out("BUSY: another check holds the worker lock"
            + ("" if slot == 1 else " for " + slot_label(slot)))
        return 0
    # A tick that finds this head superseded stops it (see the module
    # docstring); signal handlers can only be set in the main thread.
    previous = signal.signal(signal.SIGTERM, _stopped)
    # Whether this head stopped waiting: checked, moved or given up on.
    # Only then is the next tick started, since a chained tick would at
    # once pick a head that is still waiting.
    settled = False
    phases.mark("lock")
    wt = os.path.join(state_dir, slot_name("wt", slot))
    if ahead:
        # A prediction is no pull request's head: it gets no status.
        post = None
    name = who(pr, main)
    try:
        if ahead or main:
            status, detail = prepare_ahead(repo, wt, sha, run)
        else:
            status, detail = prepare(repo, wt, sha, pr, run)
        phases.mark("prepare")
        merge = {}
        if status == "ready" and MERGE_DEFAULT and not (ahead or main):
            merge = merge_default(wt, sha, pr, run)
            if "error" in merge:
                status, detail = "error", merge["error"]
        if status == "moved":
            with state_lock(state_dir):
                state = load(state_dir)
                state["waiting"].pop(sha, None)
                save(state_dir, state)
            log(state_dir, "MOVED #%d %s: the head is now %s; not checked"
                % (pr, sha[:10], detail[:10]), started, out)
            settled = True
            return 0
        if status != "ready":
            errors = count_error(state_dir, sha, ahead)
            if errors >= MAX_ERRORS:
                log(state_dir, "GAVE-UP%s %s %s after %d failures to "
                    "prepare it; the last: %s"
                    % (" AHEAD" if ahead else "", name, sha[:10], errors,
                       detail), started, out)
                if post is not None:
                    post(sha, "error", "not run: could not check the head "
                         "out %d times; the last: %s" % (errors, detail))
                settled = True
            else:
                out("NO-WORKTREE %s %s: %s" % (name, sha[:10], detail))
            return 2
        phases.mark("merge")
        if "conflicts" in merge:
            settled = True
            return conflicted(state_dir, sha, pr, merge, started, clock(),
                              post, out)
        # What is checked out and run: the head, or with MERGE_DEFAULT
        # its merge with the default branch's tip.
        checked = merge.get("commit", sha)
        if checked != sha:
            out("  merged with %s at %s as %s"
                % (MAIN, merge["base"][:10], checked[:10]))
        fresh = FRESH_WAIT is not None and DEPENDS and not main
        if fresh:
            selection = choose_fresh(wt, checked, state_dir, run)
        else:
            selection = choose(wt, checked, state_dir, run) \
                if DEPENDS and not main else None
        if selection is not None:
            out("  every step: %s" % selection["every"]
                if "every" in selection else
                "  %d of %d steps, from the record of %s"
                % (len(selection["ran"]),
                   len(selection["ran"]) + len(selection["skipped"]),
                   selection["record"][:10]) if not fresh else
                "  %d of %d steps, from %d records, the newest of %s%s"
                % (len(selection["ran"]),
                   len(selection["ran"]) + len(selection["skipped"]),
                   len(set(selection["records"].values())),
                   selection["record"][:10],
                   "; stale: " + ", ".join(selection["stale"])
                   if selection["stale"] else ""))
        phases.mark("choose")
        tree = tree_of(wt, checked, run) if TEST_AHEAD or main else None
        if tree and not ahead and not main:
            with state_lock(state_dir):
                same = tested(load(state_dir), tree, started, ok=True,
                              accept=covers(selection, lambda a, b: run(
                                  ["git", "-C", wt, "merge-base",
                                   "--is-ancestor", a, b]).returncode == 0))
            if same is not None and same != sha:
                settled = True
                return matched(state_dir, sha, pr, same, tree, started,
                               post, out)
        if main and MAIN_EVERY and tree and (not DEPENDS or os.path.exists(
                os.path.join(trace.records_dir(state_dir), tree + ".json"))):
            # designs/0011: a default-branch head whose tree a green run
            # here already passed (usually the Merger's prediction) isn't
            # run again. With depends, only once its tree has a record,
            # since writing that is what the traced run is for.
            with state_lock(state_dir):
                same = tested(load(state_dir), tree, started, ok=True)
            if same is not None and same != sha:
                settled = True
                matched(state_dir, sha, 0, same, tree, started, None, out,
                        main=True)
                if report is not None:
                    report(sha, True, [], same)
                return 0
        phases.mark("match")
        pending = None
        if post is not None:
            # Posted while the steps run: nothing waits on it but the
            # verdict's post, which must not be overwritten by it.
            pending = threading.Thread(
                target=post, daemon=True,
                args=(sha, "pending", "running%s since %s"
                      % (on_host(), iso(started))))
            pending.start()
            first_post = post

            def post(*args):
                # Every later post, the verdict's or a host fault's,
                # lands after the pending one.
                pending.join()
                return first_post(*args)
        env = step_env(state_dir, slot=slot)
        steps, wrap, tracer, files = None, None, None, {}
        composed = None
        if selection is not None and "ran" in selection:
            depends = selection["depends"]
            if fresh:
                # Each step's own record, as one file.
                depends, composed = compose(state_dir, selection, slot,
                                            started)
            env = dict(env, CHECK_BASE=selection["base"],
                       CHECK_DEPENDS=depends)
            steps = [s for s in applicable(wt) if s[0] in selection["ran"]]
        digests, reused = {}, {}
        if REUSE_AGE is not None and steps is not None:
            # designs/0009: a step whose inputs a green run here
            # already tested isn't run again.
            try:
                if composed is not None:
                    read = composed
                else:
                    with open(selection["depends"]) as handle:
                        read = json.load(handle)
            except (OSError, ValueError):
                # Pruned since it was chosen: every chosen step runs.
                read = {}
            digests = step_digests(wt, checked, read, env, run)
            reused = reuse_steps(state_dir, digests, [s[0] for s in steps],
                                 started)
            steps = [s for s in steps if s[0] not in reused]
            out("  steps: %s" % "; ".join(
                "%s %s" % (name, "reused (%s)" % reuse_text(name, reused[name])
                           if name in reused else "ran" if name in
                           selection["ran"] else "skipped")
                for name, _, _ in applicable(wt)))
        if main and DEPENDS:
            tracer = tracer_for(state_dir, run)
            if tracer is None:
                log(state_dir, "NO-TRACE %s %s: the %s tracer can't be used "
                    "here; every step runs untraced and nothing is recorded"
                    % (MAIN, sha[:10], TRACER), started, out)
            else:
                wrap = trace_wrapper(state_dir, slot, tracer, files)
        elif fresh:
            # designs/0010: a pull request's run is traced too, and when
            # green records what its steps read.
            tracer = tracer_for(state_dir, run)
            if tracer is None:
                out("  untraced: the %s tracer can't be used here; nothing "
                    "is recorded" % TRACER)
            else:
                wrap = trace_wrapper(state_dir, slot, tracer, files)
        kept = os.path.join(state_dir, RESULTS_DIR, sha)
        shutil.rmtree(kept, ignore_errors=True)
        phases.mark("setup")
        ok, results = check(wt, env, run, steps=steps,
                            prefix=memory_cap(memory_max, env, run, out),
                            wrap=wrap, kept=kept)
        phases.mark("steps")
        for r in results:
            out("  %-20s rc=%-3d %4ds" % (r["name"], r["rc"], r["seconds"]))
            if r["rc"] != 0:
                for line in r["tail"].splitlines():
                    out("    | " + line)
        finished = clock()
        for r in results:
            if "results_dropped" in r:
                log(state_dir, "RESULTS-DROPPED %s %s: %s left %d bytes in "
                    "CHECK_RESULTS, over %d; none kept"
                    % (name, sha[:10], r["name"], r["results_dropped"],
                       RESULTS_MAX), finished, out)
        if not any(r.get("results_bytes") for r in results):
            shutil.rmtree(kept, ignore_errors=True)
        fault = host_fault(results)
        if fault is not None:
            errors = count_error(state_dir, sha, ahead, "faults")
            why = "%s: %s" % fault
            for path in files.values():
                try:
                    os.unlink(path)
                except OSError:
                    pass
            if errors >= MAX_ERRORS:
                log(state_dir, "GAVE-UP%s %s %s after %d host faults; the "
                    "last: %s" % (" AHEAD" if ahead else "", name, sha[:10],
                                  errors, why), finished, out)
                if post is not None:
                    post(sha, "error", "not run: the host failed %d times; "
                         "the last: %s" % (errors, why))
                settled = True
            else:
                log(state_dir, "HOST-FAULT%s %s %s: %s; no verdict, tried "
                    "again later" % (" AHEAD" if ahead else "", name, sha[:10],
                                     why), finished, out)
            return 2
        with state_lock(state_dir):
            state = load(state_dir)
            seen = state["waiting"].pop(sha, {}).get("since", iso(started))
            record = {
                "pr": pr, "seen": seen, "started": iso(started),
                "finished": iso(finished), "ok": ok,
                "failed": [r["name"] for r in results if r["rc"] != 0],
                "seconds": sum(r["seconds"] for r in results),
                # What the fast lane's estimates come from (step_costs).
                "step_seconds": {r["name"]: r["seconds"] for r in results},
                # The same, unrounded: whole seconds lose about half a
                # second a step to anyone summing them (agent-forge #13).
                "step_wall": {r["name"]: r.get("wall", r["seconds"])
                              for r in results},
                # CPU seconds of each step's process tree: beside
                # step_wall, what was work and what was waiting.
                "step_cpu": {r["name"]: r["cpu"] for r in results
                             if "cpu" in r},
            }
            if any(r.get("results_bytes") for r in results):
                # What the steps left in CHECK_RESULTS, by step.
                record["results"] = {
                    "dir": os.path.join(RESULTS_DIR, sha),
                    "steps": {r["name"]: results_name(r["name"])
                              for r in results if r.get("results_bytes")}}
            if TEST_AHEAD:
                # What a later head with the same tree is matched by.
                record.update(tree=tree, host=socket.gethostname(),
                              steps=steps_digest())
            if digests:
                # What a later head's steps are reused by (`reusable`).
                record.update(digests=digests, host=socket.gethostname(),
                              steps=steps_digest())
            if reused:
                record["reused"] = reused
            if ahead:
                record["ahead"] = True
            if main:
                record["main"] = True
                if ok:
                    # What this slot's cache pruning keeps (prune_caches).
                    state.setdefault("cache_main", {})[str(slot)] = begun
            if selection is not None:
                record["selection"] = selection
            if merge:
                record["base"] = merge["base"]
                if checked != sha:
                    record["merged"] = checked
                tip = state["results"].get(merge["base"]) or {}
                if tip.get("main") and not tip.get("ok"):
                    record["base_red"] = True
            flaked = flakes_of(state, sha, record)
            state["results"][sha] = record
            save(state_dir, state)
        settled = True
        phases.mark("verdict")
        if main:
            log(state_dir, "%s %s %s: %s waited=%ds ran=%ds"
                % ("MAIN-GREEN" if ok else "MAIN-RED", MAIN, sha[:10],
                   verdict_word(record),
                   (started - parse(seen)).total_seconds(),
                   record["seconds"]), finished, out)
            if report is not None:
                report(sha, ok, [(r["name"], r.get("tail", ""))
                                 for r in results if r["rc"] != 0], None)
            if ok and tracer is not None:
                record_depends(state_dir, wt, sha, tree, files, env, tracer,
                               finished, out)
            else:
                # A red run records nothing: the last green record stays.
                for path in files.values():
                    try:
                        os.unlink(path)
                    except OSError:
                        pass
        else:
            log(state_dir, "VERDICT%s %s %s: %s waited=%ds ran=%ds"
                % (" AHEAD" if ahead else "", name, sha[:10],
                   verdict_word(record),
                   (started - parse(seen)).total_seconds(),
                   record["seconds"]), finished, out)
            if ok and tracer is not None:
                record_run(state_dir, wt, sha, checked,
                           tree_of(wt, checked, run), files, env, tracer,
                           finished, selection, composed, name, out)
            else:
                for path in files.values():
                    try:
                        os.unlink(path)
                    except OSError:
                        pass
        if slot == LANE and FAST_LANE_S is not None \
                and record["seconds"] > SLOW_LANE * FAST_LANE_S:
            # Not stopped: an estimate is an estimate. Logged, so a bad
            # one can be seen.
            log(state_dir, "SLOW-LANE %s %s: ran %ds in the fast lane, over "
                "%d times its %ds; %s"
                % (name, sha[:10], record["seconds"], SLOW_LANE, FAST_LANE_S,
                   ", ".join("%s %ds" % (r["name"], r["seconds"])
                             for r in results)), finished, out)
        for other in flaked:
            red = state["results"].get(other) or {}
            log(state_dir, "FLAKE tree %s: red(%s) on #%s %s, then green on "
                "#%d %s" % (tree[:10], ",".join(red.get("failed", [])),
                            red.get("pr"), other[:10], pr, sha[:10]),
                finished, out)
        phases.mark("record")
        posted = None
        if post is not None:
            if pending is not None:
                pending.join()
                phases.mark("post-pending")
            posted = post(sha, *final_status(record))
            phases.mark("post")
        # The steps' own time is in step_seconds; what the run spent
        # around them is kept beside it (agent-forge #13).
        spent = dict(phases.spent)
        # Around the steps but not in them: preparing and keeping each
        # step's results, and starting its process.
        spent["between"] = round(max(0, spent.get("steps", 0) - sum(
            r.get("wall", r["seconds"]) for r in results)), 2)
        out("  overhead: %s" % phases.text())
        with state_lock(state_dir):
            state = load(state_dir)
            if sha in state["results"]:
                if posted is not None:
                    state["results"][sha]["posted"] = posted
                state["results"][sha]["overhead"] = spent
                save(state_dir, state)
        return 0 if ok else 1
    except Stopped:
        with state_lock(state_dir):
            state = load(state_dir)
            state["waiting"].pop(sha, None)
            save(state_dir, state)
        stopped = clock()
        log(state_dir, "STOPPED %s %s after %ds: superseded; no verdict"
            % (name, sha[:10], (stopped - started).total_seconds()),
            stopped, out)
        settled = True
        return 3
    finally:
        # A second SIGTERM must not cut the chain short. A handler, not
        # SIG_IGN, which the chained tick would inherit.
        signal.signal(signal.SIGTERM, lambda signum, frame: None)
        # Under the slot's lock, so no run in it is building meanwhile.
        prune_slot_caches(state_dir, slot, out)
        held.close()
        # After the lock is free, so the started tick can take it.
        if chain is not None and settled:
            try:
                out("CHAINED %s" % " ".join(chain()[1:]))
            except Exception as err:
                # The verdict is recorded; cron's next tick starts what
                # this could not.
                out("CHAIN-FAILED: %s: %s" % (type(err).__name__, err))
        signal.signal(signal.SIGTERM, previous)


def main(argv=None, run=sh, spawn=spawn_worker, out=print, environ=None):
    environ = os.environ if environ is None else environ
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="forge-pr-check", description=__doc__.splitlines()[0])
    parser.add_argument("--worker", metavar="SHA",
                        help="check SHA now, in this process")
    parser.add_argument("--pr", type=int,
                        help="with --worker: the pull request SHA heads")
    parser.add_argument("--slot", type=int, default=1,
                        help="with --worker: the slot to run in (default 1; "
                        "%d is the fast lane)" % LANE)
    parser.add_argument("--post", action="store_true",
                        help="post each verdict as the commit status")
    parser.add_argument("--ahead", action="store_true",
                        help="with --worker: SHA is the Merger's predicted "
                        "commit for pull request --pr, checked and recorded "
                        "but never posted (merge.test_ahead)")
    parser.add_argument("--main", action="store_true",
                        help="with --worker: SHA is the default branch's "
                        "head, run in full under the tracer and its "
                        "dependency record written (checks.depends)")
    parser.add_argument("--state-dir", default=None,
                        help="where the queue, the verdicts and the logs "
                        "live (default ~/.local/state/agent-forge/"
                        "<project>/pr-check)")
    parser.add_argument("--merger-dir", default=None,
                        help="with checks.merger_first or merge.test_ahead: "
                        "the Merger's state directory, whose next.json names "
                        "the pull request to check first and whose "
                        "ahead.json lists its predictions (default "
                        "~/.local/state/agent-forge/<project>/merger)")
    parser.add_argument("--checkout", default=environ.get(
        "FORGE_CRON_CHECKOUT"), help="the checkout kept at the default "
        "branch (default $FORGE_CRON_CHECKOUT)")
    args = parser.parse_args(argv)
    if args.worker and args.main and args.pr is None:
        args.pr = 0
    if args.worker and args.pr is None:
        parser.error("--worker needs --pr")
    if not args.checkout:
        out("NO-REPO: no --checkout, and FORGE_CRON_CHECKOUT is not set")
        return 2
    got = run(["git", "-C", os.path.expanduser(args.checkout), "rev-parse",
               "--show-toplevel"])
    repo = (got.stdout or "").strip()
    if got.returncode != 0 or not repo:
        out("NO-REPO: %s is not a git checkout" % args.checkout)
        return 2
    try:
        project = config.load(os.path.join(repo, config.CONFIG_NAME))
    except config.ConfigError as err:
        out("NO-CONFIG: %s" % err)
        return 2
    configure(project)
    global MERGER_DIR_ARG
    MERGER_DIR_ARG = args.merger_dir
    state_dir = args.state_dir or locks.state_dir(project.name, "pr-check",
                                                  environ)
    os.makedirs(state_dir, exist_ok=True)
    post = make_poster(run, out, project) if args.post else None
    if args.worker:
        chain = (lambda: start_next_tick(
            checkout=repo, state_dir=args.state_dir,
            merger_dir=args.merger_dir)) if args.post else None
        report = None
        if args.main and args.post and MAIN_RED_ISSUE is not None:
            write = make_writer(project, out)
            report = lambda sha, ok, failed, same: report_main(
                state_dir, repo, sha, ok, failed, same, write, out, run)
        return worker(repo, state_dir, args.worker, args.pr, run, out,
                      post=post, chain=chain, slot=args.slot,
                      memory_max=memory_max_from_env(environ),
                      ahead=args.ahead, main=args.main, report=report)
    token = mint(run)
    if token is None:
        out("NO-TOKEN: could not mint %s's token" % POSTER)
        return 2
    merger_dir = None
    if MERGER_FIRST:
        merger_dir = args.merger_dir or locks.state_dir(project.name,
                                                        "merger", environ)
    return tick(state_dir, token, run, spawn, out, post=post,
                slots=slots_from_env(environ), checkout=repo,
                merger_dir=merger_dir)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
